00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:43.459
He wants, I want Ted to put on a suit and he achieves that goal in the end.
他想要，我想让泰德穿上一套西装，他最后也达到了这个目标。

00:07:43.459 --> 00:07:49.579
And he also wants, and I was really struck by this and I was proud of this because people do debate Barney.
而且他还想要——我对此印象深刻，我也为此感到骄傲，因为人们确实对巴尼有争议。

00:07:49.579 --> 00:07:52.259
You would Barney play nowadays, all that kind of thing.
你会想巴尼现在会怎么演，诸如此类的事情。

00:07:52.259 --> 00:07:55.660
And Barney, his other, his only other desire.
还有巴尼，他另一个，他唯一的另一个愿望。

00:07:55.660 --> 00:07:57.620
He's not chasing a woman in that episode.
他在那一集里并没有追求女人。

00:07:57.620 --> 00:08:00.500
He's talking trash a little bit, but he really wants two things.
他稍微说了点垃圾话，但他真正想要两件事。

00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:10.420
The second of which is I want Ted to say I'm his best friend, which is the saddest and most vulnerable and sweetest kind of drive.
其中的第二件是，我想要泰德承认我是他最好的朋友，这是最悲伤、最脆弱也最甜蜜的一种驱动力。

00:08:10.420 --> 00:08:20.139
He just wants to know he's important to Ted, which makes Ted important to the audience and Marshall and Lily are like Ted's parents.
他只是想知道他对泰德很重要，这使得泰德对观众也很重要，而马歇尔和莉莉就像泰德的父母。

00:08:20.139 --> 00:08:24.620
They're the same age, but they're home on the couch waiting to hear, how was your night out, son?
他们年龄相仿，但他们坐在家里的沙发上等着听：儿子，你昨晚的夜生活过得怎么样？

00:08:24.620 --> 00:08:31.899
And so he's their son and he's important to them like he's their son and to Barney, Barney wants him to be his brother and Barney wants sole ownership over Teddy.
所以他是他们的儿子，他对他们很重要，就像他是他们的儿子一样；而对巴尼来说，巴尼想让泰德成为他的兄弟，巴尼想对泰迪拥有独占权。

00:08:31.899 --> 00:08:36.259
So jealous of me, so probably, I mean, Marshall being Ted's best friend.
所以嫉妒我，所以可能，我是说，马歇尔是泰德最好的朋友。

00:08:36.259 --> 00:08:50.700
It's not probably he knows it drives Barney so crazy that Marshall, I always liked that they fought over Ted as this kind of like prize trophy friend that always, I always thought that was very sweet.
这倒未必是，他知道这把巴尼气得够呛，因为马歇尔……我一直很喜欢他们为了争夺泰德而吵架，把他当作那种像奖杯一样的珍贵朋友，我一直觉得这非常甜蜜。

00:08:50.700 --> 00:09:00.019
And especially it's where in a weird way, you see Barney's most like at his most vulnerable is when he's pleading for Ted to declare him his best friend.
尤其是，在某种奇怪的方式下，你看到巴尼最脆弱的时候，就是他恳求泰德宣布他是他最好朋友的时候。

00:09:00.019 --> 00:09:01.940
He says it multiple times in the pilot.
他在试播集里说了好几次。

00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:03.899
I'm your best friend, Sam, your best friend.
我是你最好的朋友，山姆，我是你最好的朋友。

00:09:03.899 --> 00:09:09.980
And just like, it's so, man, I'm proud of that launch of that character because that's who he is.
就像是，天哪，我为那个角色的闪亮登场感到骄傲，因为这才是真正的他。

00:09:09.980 --> 00:09:14.379
He's vulnerable, he's a little broken and he's needy.
他是脆弱的，有点破碎的，也是有所需求的。

00:09:14.379 --> 00:09:17.299
Ted is the planner getting way ahead of himself.
泰德是个总是把事情计划得过远的规划者。

00:09:17.299 --> 00:09:18.659
Marshall and Lily just got engaged.
马歇尔和莉莉刚刚订婚。

00:09:18.659 --> 00:09:20.139
Now it's my turn, that's the plan.
现在轮到我了，这就是计划。

00:09:20.139 --> 00:09:22.220
The plan, they got, they thread the needle in that.
那个计划，他们在这上面把握得很精准。

00:09:22.220 --> 00:09:24.620
Now it's my turn, so it's gonna happen tonight.
现在轮到我了，所以这事今晚就会发生。

00:09:24.620 --> 00:09:32.299
And Marshall and Lily are this old married couple interacting with like, you know, their peers who seem 20 years younger than them.
而马歇尔和莉莉是一对老夫老妻，与那些看起来比他们年轻20岁的同龄人进行互动。

00:09:32.299 --> 00:09:33.299
It's so clear.
这太清晰了。

00:09:33.299 --> 00:09:37.539
They're kind of these three tracks going and then Robin is this fourth track that gets added.
这可以说是三条并行线，然后罗宾作为第四条线被加了进来。

00:09:37.539 --> 00:09:40.740
I was very proud of the clarity of the thing.
我为它的清晰度感到非常自豪。

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:50.809
And I wrote you this little ditty to sing to you in New York City.
我给你写了一首小曲子，要在纽约市唱给你听。

00:09:50.809 --> 00:10:00.980
We'll be right back.
我们马上回来。

00:10:00.980 --> 00:10:03.139
And now, back's the show.
好了，节目继续。

00:10:03.139 --> 00:10:12.460
Also, it's funny when you consider the origin story of each character, which then, what season was how we met everyone else or how I met everyone else?
另外，当你回想每个角色的起源故事时，这很有趣，后来，那是哪一季的《我们是怎么认识大家的》或者《我怎么认识所有人的》？

00:10:12.460 --> 00:10:15.419
How we met everyone else is season three, episode five.
《我们是怎么认识大家的》是第三季第五集。

00:10:15.419 --> 00:10:23.580
That's a great episode and what's funny to me is like, well, Ted and Marshall were freshman roommates.
那是一集很棒的剧集，有趣的是，泰德和马歇尔是大一的室友。

00:10:23.580 --> 00:10:31.779
That's like a very clear, not obvious, but it's a way one meets a good friend possibly, or an enemy.
这就像是一个非常清晰、虽然不算显而易见，但确实是一个人可能会结交好朋友的方式，或者也可能是个敌人。

00:10:31.779 --> 00:10:32.779
Yeah.
是啊。

00:10:32.779 --> 00:10:41.419
Barney started talking to Ted at a urinal at a bar and then slides into a booth and starts trying to change his whole style.
巴尼是在一家酒吧的小便池旁开始和泰德说话的，然后溜进卡座里，开始试图改变泰德的整个风格。

00:10:41.419 --> 00:10:47.740
Like, it's a very strange, he just mussels his way into this guy's life.
就像，这非常奇怪，他硬生生地闯入了这家伙的生活。

00:10:47.740 --> 00:10:48.740
He really does.
他确实这么做了。

00:10:48.740 --> 00:10:49.740
It's so aggressive.
太强势了。

00:10:49.740 --> 00:10:51.500
We're going to be friends now.
我们现在要成为朋友了。

00:10:51.500 --> 00:11:00.460
I'm going to change how you dress and how you approach women and I just think he saw him as a project, but more than that, he was like, I want to roll with this guy.
我要改变你的穿着打扮，改变你接近女性的方式，我只是觉得他把泰德当成了一个项目，但不仅于此，他当时的想法是，我想跟这家伙混在一起。

00:11:00.460 --> 00:11:06.659
I thought there was something lovely and weird about like why was everyone always fighting over Ted?
我觉得为什么每个人都在争夺泰德，这件事本身有一种可爱而又诡异的魅力。

00:11:06.659 --> 00:11:09.820
Like they wanted, they wanted to be close to this guy, which I always liked.
就像他们想要，他们想要靠近这家伙，我一直很喜欢这一点。

00:11:09.820 --> 00:11:10.820
I love it.
我太喜欢了。

00:11:10.820 --> 00:11:11.820
And you feel it.
而且你能感受到这一点。

00:11:11.820 --> 00:11:15.340
You feel it right away in the, in the pile and you feel the angel side and the devil side both want Ted.
你在试播集里立刻就能感受到这一点，而且你能感觉到天使的一面和魔鬼的一面都想要泰德。

00:11:15.340 --> 00:11:16.340
Yeah.
是啊。

00:11:16.340 --> 00:11:17.340
Ted really wants.
泰德真的很想要。

00:11:17.340 --> 00:11:18.340
I mean, you did that.
我是说，你做到了。

00:11:18.340 --> 00:11:19.340
You did that.
你做到了。

00:11:19.340 --> 00:11:20.340
You did that joke on Halloween literally, right?
你在万圣节真的开了那个玩笑，对吧？

00:11:20.340 --> 00:11:21.340
Didn't Barney as a devil?
巴尼不是扮成了恶魔吗？

00:11:21.340 --> 00:11:22.340
You dressed Barney as a devil.
你把巴尼打扮成了恶魔。

00:11:22.340 --> 00:11:25.740
Yeah, we did that because it, because we said let's make literal how we pitch the show.
对，我们之所以这么做，是因为我们说过，要把我们推销这部剧的方式变成现实。

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:27.539
I like that because it's really how we pitch the show.
我喜欢这样，因为这确实就是我们推销这部剧的方式。

00:11:27.539 --> 00:11:33.659
We said, you know, because my wife and I were kind of like Marshall and Lily, we in Carter all went to college together, we met in college.
我们当时说，你知道的，因为我和我妻子有点像马歇尔和莉莉，我和卡特一起上的大学，我们在大学里认识。

00:11:33.659 --> 00:11:36.259
And then I was in New York City in our 20s.
然后20多岁的时候我在纽约市。

00:11:36.259 --> 00:11:40.419
And Carter was like the single guy going out and having fun and like the being out until three in the morning.
而卡特就像是那种单身汉，经常出去玩得很开心，甚至在外面待到凌晨三点。

00:11:40.419 --> 00:11:47.419
And I was like, sometimes that like a bed and breakfast upstate and upstate New York in my 20s with like people in their 60s, like playing Scrabble.
而我呢，20多岁的时候，有时会想去纽约州北部的加勒比民宿那种地方，和一群60岁左右的人一起玩拼词游戏。

00:11:47.419 --> 00:11:49.820
And every now and again, we were like, this is, we're getting old.
时不时地，我们会觉得，天哪，我们老了。

00:11:49.820 --> 00:11:52.259
We're making ourselves really old, really fast.
我们把我们自己弄得老得太快了。

00:11:52.259 --> 00:11:53.899
And that just seemed funny.
这听起来就很有趣。

00:11:53.899 --> 00:12:03.899
I'd be getting calls and texts and stuff from these guys, these late showwriters that we're all out having these legendary nights and they'd be like, every now and again, I'd be like, I kind of wish I was out there.
我会接到这些家伙、这些深夜节目编剧的电话和短信，他们都在外面经历那些传奇的夜晚，而我时不时就会想，我有点希望自己也能身处其中。

00:12:03.899 --> 00:12:06.500
I mean, we played that with Marshall a little bit.
我是说，我们在马歇尔身上也稍微探讨过这一点。

00:12:06.500 --> 00:12:11.340
But Ted wants to be where, Ted wants to be doing Scrabble at the button breakfast.
但泰德想去的地方，泰德想在民宿里玩拼词游戏。

00:12:11.340 --> 00:12:15.379
It's just a funny dynamic and it feels like that's immediately there.
这真是一个有趣的动态，而且感觉这种感觉在片中立刻就展现出来了。

00:12:15.379 --> 00:12:17.779
Marshall and Lilly want Ted to find that woman.
马歇尔和莉莉希望泰德能找到那个女人。

00:12:17.779 --> 00:12:19.940
We did a whole episode about that, the front porch test, right?
我们还专门拍了一整集关于这个的内容，也就是前廊测试，对吧？

00:12:19.940 --> 00:12:23.220
We want to be growing old on a front porch playing Scrabble, drinking lemonade.
我们希望老了之后能坐在前廊上玩拼词游戏、喝着柠檬水。

00:12:23.220 --> 00:12:29.980
I see on the front porch with Ted and whoever, this TBD woman's gonna be, but Barney wants to do everything to keep up from happening.
我看到泰德和某个待定的女人坐在前廊上，但巴尼却想尽一切办法阻止这件事发生。

00:12:29.980 --> 00:12:40.340
There's also a lot about in the pilot especially, but kind of really baked into the show is how old you are and what's almost like appropriate for you to be doing at what age.
特别是在试播集中，而且可以说是贯穿整部剧的另一个重要主题，是关于你的年龄，以及你在什么年龄做什么事才算“合适”。

00:12:40.340 --> 00:12:46.460
Like, Ted is obsessed in the pilot that he's 27 and they weren't supposed to get engaged until they were 30.
比如，泰德在试播集中纠结于自己已经27岁了，而他们本来不应该在30岁之前订婚的。

00:12:46.460 --> 00:12:52.179
It's like this arbitrary thing, like no one's getting engaged until they're three and Marshall throws the whole plan off.
这就像是一件很随性的事，就像没有人应该在30岁之前订婚，而马歇尔把整个计划全打乱了。

00:12:52.179 --> 00:13:02.980
and then it throws Ted into an existential crisis and but even sometimes getting out over your skis in terms of like, you don't wanna be at the bed and breakfast playing Scrapple.
然后这把泰德推向了存在主义危机，但有时甚至有点操之过急，比如你其实并不想去民宿玩拼词游戏。

00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:05.220
Like that's gonna happen soon enough.
那种生活很快就会到来。

00:13:05.220 --> 00:13:06.379
Like don't rush it.
所以别急于求成。

00:13:06.379 --> 00:13:20.820
I think there's something Ted has such a like a motor on his life that is going and he's worried he's falling far behind, which is kind of hilarious to think about nowadays like a 27 year old single guy in New York City freaking out about that.
我觉得泰德的人生像是有个强劲的发动机在推着他走，他担心自己落后得太远，现在想来其实挺逗的——一个27岁的纽约单身汉居然为此急得要死。

00:13:20.820 --> 00:13:24.500
like I think it was a little weird then, but it's even weirder now.
我觉得那时候就有点奇怪，不过现在看来更奇怪了。

00:13:24.500 --> 00:13:25.379
It's so weird.
太奇怪了。

00:13:25.379 --> 00:13:31.419
It's so weird being like, we're basically like 50 and like looking at this 27 year old being like, I got it all planned out.
感觉真奇妙，我们现在都快50岁了，看着这个27岁的家伙一副“我都计划好了”的样子。

00:13:31.419 --> 00:13:33.980
And like I got away till I'm 13 and like, Jesus dude.
还说什么我得一直等到13岁之类的，天呐，老兄。

00:13:33.980 --> 00:13:37.620
And it never mind the fact that you also looked like such kids, all of you.
更不用说你们所有人都长得跟小孩子似的。

00:13:37.620 --> 00:13:40.259
It's so great, which might be also just off camera.
这太棒了，可能镜头外也是这样吧。

00:13:40.259 --> 00:13:42.860
We looked like children as well, a card or an eye.
我们当时也长得跟小孩似的，真是绝了。

00:13:42.860 --> 00:13:56.019
And it was, I mean, what it did feel like to you to look back and like see pilot you and be sort of assessing it through now, through these eyes and just be looking at this, did it bring you back to that moment and be like, this was a different me.
那种感觉是怎样的呢？回过头去看，看到试播集里的你自己，通过现在的眼光去审视，看着这一切，这有没有把你带回那个时刻，让你觉得：这是个不一样的我。

00:13:56.019 --> 00:13:59.340
You know, it did, oh, I mean, one, I haven't, I've had a beard for so long.
你知道吗，确实有。天呐，首先，我留胡子留了太久了。

00:13:59.340 --> 00:14:01.100
It was like, weird to see my face.
看到自己没胡子的脸感觉好奇怪。

00:14:01.100 --> 00:14:06.100
I remember that guy, like, I remember that guy.
我记得那个家伙，我是说，我记得那个家伙。

00:14:09.470 --> 00:14:20.230
He doesn't exactly feel like me or he feels like some weird avatar of me or some iteration of me that is not quite around.
他感觉不完全是我，或者说他感觉像是我的某种奇怪的化身，或者是我某个已经不在了的阶段。

00:14:20.269 --> 00:14:28.659
But I was struck, and I say this very delicately, and I don't want this to come off as self-agronizing.
但我当时深有感触——我这话得说得委婉点，可不想显得我自恋。

00:14:28.659 --> 00:14:38.059
Like, I was aware, as the show went on, that there was a lot of love for Ted, but there was also like a lot of, there were some haters, right?
随着剧集的播出，我知道很多人喜欢泰德，但也有很多人，呃，有一些黑粉，对吧？

00:14:38.059 --> 00:14:44.019
And I was new to fame, I was new to being seen every week in people's homes.
而我当时对成名还很生疏，对每周都被人在家里看到也感到很不习惯。

00:14:44.019 --> 00:14:49.259
So I, and I'm a sensitive person, like I'm pretty thin skinned, I don't think I'm alone in that.
所以我，而且我是个性感敏感的人，我的脸皮挺薄的，我想我不是唯一这样的人。

00:14:49.259 --> 00:15:04.019
You know, getting trolled or getting, you know, and I didn't get abused in like a, you know, some sort of epic way, but I think I went through the series feeling, I know what I'm good at here.
你知道的，遭到网暴或者其他什么的，虽然我没有经历过那种史诗级的恶劣网暴，但整个系列拍下来，我感觉自己一直是带着一种“我知道自己在这里擅长什么”的心态在演。

00:15:04.019 --> 00:15:05.019
Yeah.
对。

00:15:05.019 --> 00:15:17.340
And I also know that sometimes Ted's eccentricities annoy people or whatever, but I watched the pilot and I was struck by the fact that I thought I was really good in it and I hope it's okay to say that.
我也知道有时泰德的古怪性格会惹恼别人或者怎样，但我看了试播集后，我印象深刻的是，我觉得自己在里面演得真好，希望这样说没问题。

00:15:17.340 --> 00:15:18.340
You were.
你确实演得很好。

00:15:18.340 --> 00:15:25.163
I thought there was a job to do with this guy that was that was kind of a high level of difficulty, right?
我觉得这个角色有一项任务，那就是难度挺高的，对吧？

00:15:25.163 --> 00:15:37.458
To be like very high, the kind of engaging center, charming center of the show, to be the eyes and ears for the audience, but also to be passionate and lovesick and forward, you know, moving.
要成为整部剧非常有吸引力的中心、迷人的中心，要成为观众的眼睛和耳朵，但同时又要充满激情、为情所困并且勇往直前。

00:15:37.458 --> 00:15:43.251
Like there was a big ask and I thought, you know what, you did really, you did a good job.
这要求可不低，而我想，你知道吗，你真的做到了，你演得很好。

00:15:43.251 --> 00:15:49.109
Like it was almost like telling a younger part of me, but you were really hard on yourself.
这感觉就像是在对过去那个更年轻的自己说：其实你对自己太苛刻了。

00:15:49.109 --> 00:15:50.397
You did a great job.
你干得太棒了。

00:15:50.397 --> 00:15:53.422
See, this is what this podcast is a little bit.
你看，这就是这个播客的意义所在。

00:15:53.422 --> 00:15:56.576
I hope people can hear that and think about that.
我希望大家能听到这些，并对此有所思考。

00:15:56.576 --> 00:15:58.121
Really think about that.
真的好好想一想。

00:15:58.121 --> 00:16:01.790
It's taken on 20 years for you to look back and say that.
你花了20年的时间才回过头来说出这句话。

00:16:01.790 --> 00:16:05.459
It's almost like most high schoolers are pretty insecure.
这就好比大多数高中生都挺没有安全感的。

00:16:06.330 --> 00:16:09.082
And most high schoolers are looking at themselves.
大多数高中生在审视自己时。

00:16:09.082 --> 00:16:12.496
Three years earlier, you look like a totally different person.
仅仅三年之前，你看上去就像个完全不同的人。

00:16:12.496 --> 00:16:16.570
And you're adapting to this new body and this new voice and new body hair.
你要适应这个新身体、新嗓音还有新的体毛。

00:16:16.570 --> 00:16:20.090
and like your body is changing for everyone.
而且对每个人来说，你的身体都在发生变化。

00:16:20.090 --> 00:16:23.730
And it's a very startling experience.
这是一段非常让人惊慌失措的经历。

00:16:23.730 --> 00:16:28.929
And then you look back on pictures and you're like, you know, we were pretty like attractive young kids.
然后你再看以前的照片，就会觉得，你知道吗，我们当时其实挺像几个有魅力的年轻小伙的。

00:16:28.929 --> 00:16:31.049
We were really hard on ourselves, right?
我们对自己真的太苛刻了，对吧？

00:16:31.049 --> 00:16:31.889
Yeah, yeah, yeah.
对，对，对。

00:16:31.889 --> 00:16:34.570
That's how I felt watching the pilot like, man, I'm so hard on myself.
我看了试播集也是这种感觉，天呐，我对自己的要求太高了。

00:16:34.570 --> 00:16:35.769
I looked fun.
我看起来状态不错。

00:16:35.769 --> 00:16:40.049
I looked like a guy who belonged, you know?
我看起来就像个本该如此的家伙，你知道吗？

00:16:40.049 --> 00:16:42.970
I looked like a guy who belonged on the TV, you know?
我看起来就像个本该上电视的家伙，你知道吗？

00:16:42.970 --> 00:16:48.710
I felt like you were so self-assured in the role and very, very confident.
我觉得你在角色里表现得非常自信，而且信心十足。

00:16:48.710 --> 00:16:52.190
I mean, some of that was also that Ted's high status in that pilot.
我的意思是，部分原因也是因为泰德在那个试播集里处于高地位。

00:16:52.190 --> 00:16:53.950
Everyone knows what's about to be a Ted.
每个人都知道接下来会有一个怎样的泰德。

00:16:53.950 --> 00:16:54.950
Everyone knows what's about to be a Ted.
每个人都知道接下来会有一个怎样的泰德。

00:16:54.950 --> 00:17:00.230
I also think you guys wrote in and we're going to unpack this like ad nauseam so I don't want to get too much into it.
我也觉得你们写了一些——我们以后会像没完没了般细细剖析这一点，所以我就不多说了。

00:17:00.230 --> 00:17:13.789
But you wrote in some, one of the things that I struggled with the first season and I remember talking to you in Carter about this, is there'd be an episode like drum roll please where Ted was absolutely the hero.
但你们写了一些情节，我在第一季时感到很纠结，我记得跟你在卡特谈过这个，就是会有类似《击鼓传花》这样的单集，在剧里泰德绝对是英雄。

00:17:13.789 --> 00:17:19.190
He was the romantic hero and he was held up and almost shot in a heroic way.
他是浪漫英雄，被大家高高捧起，甚至带有一种英雄主义的光环。

00:17:19.190 --> 00:17:21.349
And you were supposed to be rooting for him.
而且你是应该支持他的。

00:17:21.349 --> 00:17:30.710
The very next episode, I think it was like, he made like the mixtape for Ashley, maybe, or something like, it was something so silly.
但紧接着下一集，我想大概是，他为罗宾还是谁制作了混音带之类的，或者别的什么事，那是件非常蠢的事情。

00:17:30.710 --> 00:17:35.150
And it was like, I could tell that there was an invitation then to make fun of him.
这就让人觉得，剧集当时其实发出了一个邀请，让大家去嘲笑他。

00:17:35.150 --> 00:17:43.950
And I remember saying to you guys, I'm getting whiplash going from like, I'm a hero to, I'm a ridiculous person to be ridiculed.
我记得我对你们说过，我感觉自己像得了颈椎病一样，一会儿是英雄，一会儿又成了个滑稽到该被嘲笑的人。

00:17:43.950 --> 00:18:00.470
So it's a thing I want to talk about, it's a thing I want to talk about, I mean, it's a thing I want to say, I want to just play like Jack Bauer or someone like who is just always like a hero of the same job, that's what I'm saying.
所以这是我想谈的一点，我想说的是，我其实只想演像杰克·鲍尔那样的人，那种一直都是英雄的常规角色，这就是我想说的。

00:18:00.470 --> 00:18:18.923
But I did, I do feel in the pilot, even though Ted has his foibles, he has the kind of heroes journey, momentum, you're rooting for him, which I did find, I do, we can talk about this in a second, but like, I was so into him being with Robin when he goes up in the suit.
不过我的确觉得在试播集中，尽管泰德有他的小缺点，但他有那种英雄之旅的势头，你会支持他，我确实发现，我们待会儿可以聊聊这个，但我当时太迷恋他和罗宾在一起的那一幕了，当他坐着升降机上去的时候。

00:18:18.923 --> 00:18:25.962
And it's really quite sexy for prime time, just in terms of like the way they're looking at each other.
对于黄金档电视剧来说这真的很性感，尤其是他们看着彼此的那种眼神。

00:18:25.962 --> 00:18:40.520
Like there's real bad room eyes and there's real like, and the moment he's, I knew it was coming, but the moment he says, I think I'm in love with you, I almost was like, like, Barney Marshall and Lily, like what?
那种眼神里有着浓浓的卧室暧昧，而且就在他——我知道接下来要发生什么，但当他说出“我想我爱上你了”那一刻，我简直就像——就像巴尼、马歇尔和莉莉那样，心里想：什么鬼？

00:18:40.520 --> 00:18:42.433
Like it was such a bad move.
这简直是个烂透了的举动。

00:18:42.433 --> 00:18:50.429
And of course, it sets off the entire series, so it needed to happen, but it was such a ill-timed, ill-informed move.
当然，这也引发了整部剧的故事，所以这是必须要发生的，但这是一个时机不对、不明智的举动。

00:18:50.430 --> 00:18:54.670
So I was actually crushed by that in a way that I was like, okay, this pilot's working.
所以我当时真的被那一幕整破防了，以至于我觉得：好吧，这个试播集成功了。

00:18:54.670 --> 00:18:57.869
Like if you're mad at him for reading that.
因为你会对他说出那句话感到生气。

00:18:57.869 --> 00:19:04.430
I think the show called it shots so early to not just be like, this is the, it all makes sense half-hour.
我觉得这部剧很早就亮出了底牌，它不只是那种波澜不惊、半小时就能看懂的剧。

00:19:04.430 --> 00:19:05.430
Right.
没错。

00:19:05.430 --> 00:19:10.630
It's like the other things at the, the show, right before us on CBS and the show right after us are that.
就像CBS在我们之前播出的剧和在我们之后播出的剧就是那样。

00:19:10.630 --> 00:19:11.630
And the show after that.
还有之后的剧也是。

00:19:11.630 --> 00:19:47.406
the other shows were that because they were these sort of like goes down easy kind of like simple comedies and that was what that's the thing that's so hard to explain to people because we were this weird black sheep on that network but we called that was the other thing that's proud of in that pilot we called our shot like that moment Josh where he says I love you and you cut to you time jump to three different timelines of people saying what you jump to right when you have robins say what you jump to the future kids 25 years in the future saying what And you also jumped to the future group, all saying what, right, being told the story, like being told the story of that later.
其他剧之所以那样，是因为它们是那种容易消化的、简单的喜剧，而这正是最难向人们解释的地方，因为我们在那个电视网上就像是个奇怪的替罪羊，但这就是我们在试播集中感到骄傲的另一件事，我们亮出了底牌，就像乔希说的那个时刻，他说“我爱你”，然后镜头立刻剪辑到——时间跳跃到三个不同的时间线，人们异口同声地说着“什么”——你跳到了罗宾说“什么”的镜头，你跳到了25年后的未来孩子们的镜头，他们也说着“什么”。你还跳到了未来的朋友们那里，大家都在说“什么”，对吧，正听着这个故事，就像在听后来发生的这个故事一样。

00:19:47.406 --> 00:19:48.866
And that's boom, boom, boom.
这真是砰、砰、砰。

00:19:48.866 --> 00:19:53.769
In like 10 seconds, there's three timelines, one of which is literally 25 years in the future.
在大约10秒钟内，出现了三条时间线，其中一条甚至直白地设定在25年之后。

00:19:53.769 --> 00:19:54.269
Yeah.
对。

00:19:54.029 --> 00:19:58.109
And I, that was like, maybe my favorite moment rewatching a pilot because that was like, yeah.
而那简直是我重看试播集时最喜欢的时刻，因为感觉太棒了。

00:19:58.109 --> 00:19:58.819
It's so jarring.
那种感觉太震撼了。

00:19:58.819 --> 00:20:05.470
And I remember having, I don't know if you remember this, but we would get, we, we had notes and concerns on that moment from the studio in that work.
我记得当时——我不知道你还记不记得，但当时电视台和电视网对那个镜头提出过意见和担忧。

00:20:05.470 --> 00:20:07.675
They were like, is that just too confusing?
他们问：这样会不会太让人困惑了？

00:20:07.675 --> 00:20:12.239
Because you're, the, the one that was deemed most confusing was jumping ahead to the bar.
因为在他们看来，最让人困惑的就是直接跳跃到酒吧的场景。

00:20:12.239 --> 00:20:13.624
You know what I mean later?
你知道我意思吧，就是后面那个场景？

00:20:13.624 --> 00:20:15.163
And it's the gang saying what?
也就是那帮朋友们说“什么”的场景。

00:20:15.163 --> 00:20:21.164
Because there were like, do people's, are people's brains going to catch up to that idea that we're jumping to there?
因为他们当时觉得：观众的大脑能跟得上这个概念吗？我们这是直接跳跃到未来了？

00:20:21.164 --> 00:20:23.164
They're talking about it in the future.
他们是在未来谈论这件事。

00:20:23.164 --> 00:20:26.138
It's my favorite quote about filmmaking from Billy Wilder.
我最喜欢比利·怀尔德关于电影制作的名言。

00:20:26.138 --> 00:20:29.985
He says, let the audience put two and two together in the love you forever.
他说，让观众自己把线索拼凑起来，那就是永远的爱。

00:20:29.985 --> 00:20:30.485
Yes.
没错。

00:20:30.485 --> 00:20:33.511
And that's, that is what that pilot did at a few key spots.
这正是那个试播集在几个关键地方所做到的。

00:20:33.511 --> 00:20:35.101
So the, so the I love you spot.
所以在“我爱你”这个节点。

00:20:35.101 --> 00:20:36.486
Ted has, has been the hero.
泰德一直都是个英雄。

00:20:36.486 --> 00:20:38.024
He's having his hero's moment.
他正经历着属于他的英雄时刻。

00:20:38.024 --> 00:20:46.948
He absolutely fucks it up in an incredibly jarring, like nails on a chalkboard, cringy way, but even made you yourself 20 something years later, go, no, and scream at the TV.
然后他以一种极其震撼、像用指甲刮黑板那样令人尴尬的方式彻底搞砸了，甚至让你在20多年后回看时，依然会忍不住说“不”，对着电视大喊大叫。

00:20:46.948 --> 00:20:51.769
I think you texted me that and you're like, I'm yelling a tad on the TV right now with Janana.
我想你当时还发信息给我，说你正在跟珍娜一起对着电视里的泰德大喊大叫。

00:20:51.769 --> 00:20:57.309
And yet that spurs you into that great little boom, boom, boom, three different timelines, jump, jump, jump.
然而正是这一点，激发出了那个精彩绝伦的砰、砰、砰，三个不同的时间线，不断地跳跃、跳跃、跳跃。

00:20:57.309 --> 00:21:00.745
And that was the show saying, this is what the, this is who we are.
这就是剧集在说：这就是我们，这就是我们是谁。

00:21:00.745 --> 00:21:01.360
Yeah, it is.
是的，确实如此。

00:21:01.360 --> 00:21:05.463
But pilot is challenging because you have to teach the audience how to watch it.
但试播集很有挑战性，因为你必须教观众如何去欣赏它。

00:21:05.463 --> 00:21:10.284
And especially if it's a show that that is so as narratively complex is how I met your mother.
尤其是当这是一部叙事如此复杂的剧，就像《老爸老妈浪漫史》这样。

00:21:09.990 --> 00:21:15.910
You had to do a lot of like training of your audience to watch the show.
你必须对观众进行大量的“训练”让他们来看这部剧。

00:21:15.910 --> 00:21:17.150
And that was our argument to keep it.
这就是我们坚持保留这个设计的理由。

00:21:17.150 --> 00:21:20.509
We said, let's keep it because this will show people that we're going to be jumping around all the time.
我们说：一定要保留它，因为这能向大家展示，我们以后会一直在不同的时空中跳跃。

00:21:20.509 --> 00:21:21.829
It'll keep you on your toes.
这会让你时刻保持专注。

00:21:21.829 --> 00:21:25.029
This is not like lie back on the couch looking at your phone and watching.
这不是那种可以躺在沙发上刷着手机看的剧。

00:21:25.029 --> 00:21:26.549
This is like pale a little bit of attention here.
这需要你在这里集中一点注意力。

00:21:26.549 --> 00:21:27.670
And it's to their credit.
这确实得归功于他们。

00:21:27.670 --> 00:21:28.670
The student network said, great.
学生网说，太棒了。

00:21:28.670 --> 00:21:29.670
Keep it.
保持这样。

00:21:29.670 --> 00:21:30.670
Let's go.
我们走吧。

00:21:30.670 --> 00:21:36.069
I did want to go back to one thing you said about we're not the tidy resolution show.
我确实想回到你刚才说的一点，就是我们不是那种大团圆结局的节目。

00:21:36.069 --> 00:21:36.569
Yeah.
对。

00:21:36.569 --> 00:21:43.968
I wonder if some of the ferocious dedication to the show is almost like that friend you have that tells you the truth.
我在想，对这部剧近乎狂热的喜爱，是不是有点像你身边的某个朋友，总是对你讲真话。

00:21:43.968 --> 00:21:44.468
Yeah.
对。

00:21:44.468 --> 00:21:50.613
Is like you're closer to the person who will say the thing or say the thing because they love you.
就像你和那种敢于直言、或者因为爱你而对你说实话的人会更亲近。

00:21:50.613 --> 00:21:51.113
Yeah.
对。

00:21:51.113 --> 00:21:59.138
You know, my friend John, John Morrow, who shout out to John who did our art for how he made your mother and he does all my art.
你知道，我的朋友约翰，约翰·莫罗，在这里向约翰致敬，他为《老爸老妈浪漫史》设计了美术，而且他包办了我所有的美术设计。

00:21:59.138 --> 00:22:10.048
But he said to me one year, what years ago, and I don't think I'll mind me saying this, but he said, hey, you've known me for a long time now, like, where are my blind spots?
但几年前的某一年，他跟我说——我觉得他不会介意我这么说——他说，嘿，你认识我挺久了，像我的盲点在哪？

00:22:10.048 --> 00:22:12.995
Like, what am I missing as a person in my life?
比如说，作为生活中的一个人，我忽略了什么？

00:22:12.995 --> 00:22:15.189
And he really wanted that feedback.
他是真的很想要这种反馈。

00:22:15.189 --> 00:22:19.704
And then I asked for it back, you know, just because I really trust him.
后来我也向他要了反馈，你知道的，纯粹是因为我真的很信任他。

00:22:19.704 --> 00:22:22.149
And it was all said with a lot of love.
而且这些话都是带着满满的爱意说出来的。

00:22:21.589 --> 00:22:29.736
But there was a, this author, Saeed Bloom, who I've become friends with, he has a book out called the Five Types of Wealth.
不过，有这么一位作家，赛义德·布鲁姆，我和他成了朋友，他出了一本书叫《五种财富》。

00:22:29.736 --> 00:22:36.160
And Jordana and I went to hear it at the 92nd Street Wise, Susan Kane interviewed him last night.
乔丹娜和我去92街 Y 听了分享，苏珊·凯恩昨晚采访了他。

00:22:36.160 --> 00:22:38.412
It was a really wonderful evening.
那真是一个美好的夜晚。

00:22:38.412 --> 00:22:40.796
But he said this really great thing.
但他说了个特别棒的观点。

00:22:40.796 --> 00:22:47.088
He said, if your life is a movie, what is the audience shouting at you that you're not getting?
他说，如果你的生活是一部电影，观众在朝你大喊什么，而你却没有意识到？

00:22:47.088 --> 00:22:50.068
You know, it's like, he loves you, you idiot.
你知道，这就好像在说，他爱你啊，你个笨蛋。

00:22:50.068 --> 00:22:53.910
Like, what is Don't say I love you on the first date, Ted.
就像是，别在第一次约会时说我爱你，泰德。

00:22:53.910 --> 00:23:10.089
Yeah, but it was funny because that's the energy of you know, I was doing what you were having the you were having characters at various times due to the thing that the audience would do, which is like, wait, what, you know, yeah, yeah, absolutely, absolutely.
对，但这就很有趣，因为那种活力——你知道的，我当时在做的事，就是让你在不同的时刻让角色做出那种会让观众想要大喊的举动，比如，等等，什么，你知道的，对，对，完全是，完全是。

00:23:10.089 --> 00:23:16.630
And that what, what, what was also our way of acknowledging that like we're asking the audience to go along with something here that they're going to be yelling about.
而且这也、这也就是我们的一种方式，承认我们在让观众跟随着一段剧情，而这段剧情会让他们忍不住大喊。

00:23:16.630 --> 00:23:21.130
So we need people yelling literally throughout 25 was 25 years of yelling at Ted's.
所以我们需要有人在字面意义上一直大喊，贯穿泰德25年来的生活——是25年对着泰德大喊。

00:23:21.130 --> 00:23:26.490
That's all I was like saying, look, we're outraged too, we're outraged because we wrote.
我当时只是想说，看吧，我们也很愤怒，我们愤怒是因为我们写了这些。

00:23:26.490 --> 00:23:30.569
People are still going to be yelling at this in the year 2030, the kids yelling at him.
直到2030年，人们还是会对着这个大喊，孩子们还是会对嚷嚷。

00:23:30.569 --> 00:23:33.809
Yeah, it's an iconic thing.
对，这是一个标志性的东西。

00:23:33.809 --> 00:23:36.329
Let me just ask you a question from the internet about this.
让我来问你一个来自网上的关于这方面的问题。

00:23:36.329 --> 00:23:47.089
Alex Lamar, we've got a lot of questions that have come in and please send them everywhere that you send questions, but we're all over online on our website, on Instagram.
亚历克斯·拉马尔，我们收到了很多提问，请大家继续通过各种渠道发送问题，我们在网上的各个地方都有阵地，我们的网站上，还有 Instagram 上。

00:23:47.089 --> 00:23:47.589
And H.
然后 H.

00:23:47.589 --> 00:24:09.692
Shane 03 asks you, was it difficult to first get into playing Ted, or did you relate to the character and did that make it easy to do so while you're looking back on this, this, this character that again is so positive, so forward thinking, were you that at 27 and how do those two connect?
Shane 03 问你，一开始出演泰德很难吗？还是说你和这个角色很有共鸣，这让扮演他变得容易？当你回看这个角色——一个如此积极、如此具有前瞻性的角色时，你在27岁的时候也是这样吗？这两个状态是如何联系在一起的？

00:24:09.650 --> 00:24:10.650
Yeah.
对。

00:24:10.650 --> 00:24:14.130
I mean, I was what 3031 when we did the pilot, I can't remember.
我是说，我们拍试播集的时候我大概30、31岁，记不太清了。

00:24:14.130 --> 00:24:16.529
But so I'm three or four years older than Ted.
所以我比泰德大三四岁。

00:24:16.529 --> 00:24:19.849
I always felt like I was playing someone younger than me.
我总觉得我演的是一个比我小的人。

00:24:19.849 --> 00:24:24.099
So it was almost like my little brother.
所以它几乎就像是我的小弟弟。

00:24:24.099 --> 00:24:32.140
I've described him as like my little brother that I love dearly, but sometimes I wanted to smack around and tell him to snap out of it or be a little different.
我曾把他形容为我深爱的小弟弟，但有时我很想狠狠拍他一巴掌，叫他清醒一点，或者稍微改变一下。

00:24:32.140 --> 00:24:32.980
I've never heard you say it that way.
我从没听你这么说过。

00:24:32.980 --> 00:24:34.579
That's a fantastic description.
这个形容太棒了。

00:24:36.059 --> 00:24:41.059
I think I shared some of Ted's ambition and pluck.
我想我和泰德有一些共同的野心和干劲。

00:24:43.259 --> 00:24:52.420
I do think at that time, I was much more, I wouldn't say I was more optimistic, I was, there's a term called spiritual bypassing.
我确实觉得在那个时候，我更加——我不会说我更乐观，我当时处于一种叫做“精神绕行”的状态。

00:24:52.420 --> 00:25:01.940
I don't know if you guys have ever heard that, but it's basically like always kind of moving through and looking at the bright side and it's a very shadow denying kind of posture.
我不知道你们有没有听说过这个词，但它基本上就是那种总是敷衍了事、只看光明面的一面，是一种非常否定阴暗面的姿态。

00:25:01.940 --> 00:25:27.243
And I think in some ways, in this is like Ted, like I hadn't, throughout the course of the run, throughout my 30s, like I needed more life, I needed more suffering, I needed more failure, I needed, you know, when I had failures along the way, I got fired from my first pilot, which I've talked about, like I need, but there's something about you want, you get seasoned life seasons you, you know?
我觉得在某些方面，就像泰德一样，我当时并没有经历过——在剧集播出的过程中，在我的30多岁里，我需要更多的人生阅历，我需要更多的痛苦，我需要更多的失败，我需要，你知道的，当我一路上遭遇失败时，我第一部拍的试播集就被炒了鱿鱼，我之前也聊过这个，我需要——但有些东西就是你想要的，生活的阅历会让你变得成熟，对吧？

00:25:27.243 --> 00:25:33.122
And I don't think we have to engineer stress and strain and set back and stumble for people.
而且我不认为我们必须人为地为人们制造压力、挫折、阻碍和跌倒。

00:25:33.122 --> 00:25:34.528
Life does that, right?
生活自然会带来这些，对吧？

00:25:34.528 --> 00:25:37.339
But I think I had some naivete a little bit.
但我认为我当时确实有点天真。

00:25:38.460 --> 00:25:41.523
I think I didn't quite understand what it was.
我觉得我当时并不是很理解那意味着什么。

00:25:41.523 --> 00:25:46.717
I think if I had a little more confidence in the pilot than I did maybe later.
我觉得我在拍试播集时的自信可能比后来还要多一点。

00:25:46.717 --> 00:25:50.713
And I know I regained it at some level but or at some point.
我知道我在某种程度上或者在某个时间点重新找回了它。

00:25:50.713 --> 00:25:54.642
But there was something I was so such a driven young actor.
但当时有些东西让我觉得自己是一个极其有冲劲的年轻演员。

00:25:54.642 --> 00:25:57.372
I really knew this was a big opportunity.
我真的很清楚这是一个巨大的机会。

00:25:57.372 --> 00:25:59.237
I really wanted to seize it.
我真的想抓住它。

00:25:59.237 --> 00:26:01.900
But, you know, I lived in New York City.
但是，你知道，我住在纽约市。

00:26:01.900 --> 00:26:03.898
I drank a lot of beer at bars.
我在酒吧里喝了很多啤酒。

00:26:03.898 --> 00:26:09.692
I got scared to talk to women and some nights it worked and some nights it didn't work.
我和女人说话会感到害怕，有些晚上成功了，有些晚上没成功。

00:26:09.692 --> 00:26:13.288
You know, like I went to a small liberal arts college.
你知道的，就像我上了一所小型文理学院。

00:26:13.288 --> 00:26:14.353
I was from Ohio.
我来自俄亥俄州。

00:26:14.353 --> 00:26:18.282
Like there were a lot of biographical things that lined up.
有很多个人经历上的契合点。

00:26:18.282 --> 00:26:23.743
But I also think I had, I constitutionally, I think have, I don't know what it is.
但我也觉得我从骨子里，我身上有——我不知道那是什么。

00:26:23.743 --> 00:26:29.137
I went on a very spiritual journey when I was in my 30s on, you know, off screen.
我在30多岁的时候经历了一段非常深刻的精神之旅，是在镜头之外。

00:26:29.137 --> 00:26:43.655
And I was really trying to ask myself some big questions because I think what happens to me, and this is something we could talk about later, but like when you have success, you get to the kind of top of that mountain.
我真的试图问自己一些宏大的问题，因为我觉得发生在我身上的事——这是我们以后可以讨论的事情——当你取得成功时，你登上了那座山的山顶。

00:26:43.655 --> 00:26:51.379
It's the thing of like, you climb to the top of the ladder and realize, maybe it was propped against the wrong wall.
这就好像是，你费力爬到了梯子的顶端，却发现，也许这个梯子靠错了墙。

00:26:51.940 --> 00:26:53.717
You know, like, is this what I want?
你知道的，就像，这真的是我想要的吗？

00:26:53.717 --> 00:26:55.148
Is this what I'm looking for?
这真的是我在寻找的吗？

00:26:55.148 --> 00:26:59.048
And so I really started to ask myself some big questions about, is this enough?
所以，我真的开始问自己一些关于“这就够了吗”的宏大问题。

00:26:59.048 --> 00:27:01.220
You know, what do I really want to be doing?
你知道的，我真正想做的是什么？

00:27:02.259 --> 00:27:05.128
And maybe those are, you know, not unmosby-ish.
也许这些问题，你知道的，也挺有泰德·莫斯比的风格。

00:27:05.128 --> 00:27:10.498
But I think I appreciated his energy and his passion in his optimism and his resilience.
但我真的很欣赏他的活力、他的激情、他的乐观以及他的韧性。

00:27:10.498 --> 00:27:20.079
I mean, that would be the biggest thing that I would almost like to learn from him and honor about him was, man, that guy got kicked down and just came back.
我是说，这几乎是我最想向他学习、最想赞美他的地方，那就是，天哪，这家伙被生活狠狠踢倒后，居然还能爬起来。

00:27:20.079 --> 00:27:20.872
He came back.
他又站起来了。

00:27:20.872 --> 00:27:22.825
And that's, that's a great word.
而这，这是一个绝佳的词。

00:27:22.825 --> 00:27:23.325
Yeah.
对。

00:27:23.325 --> 00:27:25.094
Resilience and vulnerability.
韧性与脆弱性。

00:27:24.900 --> 00:27:28.715
You know, he was not, he was not afraid to say how he was feeling.
你知道，他从不、从不害怕表达自己的真实感受。

00:27:28.715 --> 00:27:31.547
He was not afraid to, um, well, that's the thing.
他从不害怕，嗯，这就是关键所在。

00:27:31.547 --> 00:27:33.743
I think it heroic qualities for a man.
我认为这是一个男人身上具有英雄主义特质的品质。

00:27:33.743 --> 00:27:34.243
I agree.
我赞同。

00:27:34.243 --> 00:27:37.191
And I don't think you were seeing that in comedies.
而且我觉得你在喜剧里看不到这种特质。

00:27:37.191 --> 00:27:39.793
It's like you weren't seeing a man sensitive.
就好像你看不到一个敏感的男人。

00:27:39.793 --> 00:27:42.567
It's like the speech Ted gives after fucking up.
就像泰德在搞砸之后发表的那番演讲。

00:27:42.567 --> 00:27:51.238
After, say, blurting out I love you up in Robbins, you don't get that amazing speech from Ted and so wonderfully acted and performed by you out there.
比如，在31冰激凌脱口而出“我爱你”之后，你不会从泰德那里听到那段精彩绝伦的独白，也不会看到你在镜头前如此美妙的演技和表现。

00:27:51.238 --> 00:27:53.608
I mean, that is, that is just a home run.
我是说，那绝对是一个全垒打。

00:27:53.608 --> 00:27:56.151
I was like tears in my eyes rewatching that.
我重温那一幕时简直热泪盈眶。

00:27:56.151 --> 00:27:58.463
In part just because of how good it was.
一部分是因为它演得太好了。

00:27:58.463 --> 00:28:04.937
And in part just remembering being there that night watching you do it on on that street at the CBS Radford Lot.
另一部分则是记起了当晚在 CBS 雷德福片场的街道上看着你表演的情景。

00:28:04.900 --> 00:28:07.740
That was not where we shot, how I met your mother after the pilot.
那并不是我们拍试播集之后拍摄《老爸老妈浪漫史》的地方。

00:28:07.740 --> 00:28:15.740
We shot the pilot in the valley of the CBS Lot, and then shot every other episode, basically at the Fox Lot in West LA, so it was completely different.
我们在 CBS 片场的山谷里拍了试播集，然后基本上每一集都在西洛杉矶的福克斯片场拍，所以完全不一样。

00:28:15.740 --> 00:28:23.140
But for the pilot, for CBS, that's where we had the space and the money sent us there at that moment.
但对于试播集的拍摄，对于 CBS 来说，我们在那个时刻拥有那片场地和资金支持。

00:28:23.140 --> 00:28:28.019
And I had this memory, Josh, of you out there in the magic of that night.
乔治，我脑海中一直留存着那个记忆，关于你置身于那个夜晚的魔幻氛围中的样子。

00:28:28.019 --> 00:28:32.940
And it was a night shoot on the fake New York City Street, but it looked beautiful.
那是那条虚构的纽约街道上的一场夜戏，但看起来美极了。

00:28:32.940 --> 00:28:34.180
It was all lit up all pretty.
整条街都亮起了灯，非常漂亮。

00:28:34.180 --> 00:28:37.740
The, the, you know, there's that thing where they've kind of went down the sidewalks.
你知道的，人行道上还有那种灯光效果。

00:28:37.740 --> 00:28:39.980
So, like listening and shiny and magical.
所以，看起来闪闪发光，充满魔力。

00:28:39.980 --> 00:28:47.220
And just watching you there, it's like I stepped into that TV now from the year 2025 back into 2005.
就只是看着你在那里，感觉就像我从2025年穿越回了2005年，走进了那台电视机里。

00:28:47.220 --> 00:28:51.740
And I felt I was there with young you and young me.
我觉得自己和年轻的你、年轻的我都在那里。

00:28:51.740 --> 00:28:56.779
And I had this unbelievable wave of nostalgia that I was like, overcome by.
我涌起了一股难以置信的怀旧之情，整个人都被淹没了。

00:28:56.779 --> 00:28:59.220
And I was like, I wanted to go be with some.
当时我就想，我想去和某人在一起。

00:28:59.220 --> 00:29:00.539
I wanted to go be there.
我想去那个地方。

00:29:00.539 --> 00:29:02.579
I wanted to feel what they felt.
我想去感受他们所感受的一切。

00:29:02.579 --> 00:29:11.440
There was this drumming aliveness I felt of like, yes, in our late 20s or 30s, we were 30, I think.
我当时能感觉到一种充满生机的跳动感，就是，对，在我们快三十岁或三十岁的时候，我想我们当时30岁。

00:29:11.440 --> 00:29:14.519
And there we were, this was our first big shot.
我们就那样站在那里，这是我们的第一次大好机会。

00:29:14.519 --> 00:29:17.880
And we were all vibing and aligned and connected.
我们都很有默契，步调一致，心连着心。

00:29:17.880 --> 00:29:19.200
We knew what we were doing.
我们知道自己在做什么。

00:29:19.200 --> 00:29:24.799
I remember watching you do that speech, which, again, is so brilliantly performed, brilliantly written.
我记得看着你演那段独白，真的，表演得太精彩了，台词写得也太棒了。

00:29:24.799 --> 00:29:28.799
I give Carter so much, like Carter really wrote, he wrote that speech so beautifully.
我特别感激卡特，卡特真的写了，他把那段独白写得太优美了。

00:29:28.799 --> 00:29:30.519
I feel like that's such a Carter.
我觉得那太典型卡特了。

00:29:30.519 --> 00:29:34.039
That was really Carter connecting with Ted, I think, because the Carter was single.
我认为那真的是卡特在与特德心灵相通，因为当时卡特还是单身。

00:29:34.039 --> 00:29:35.359
Carter was saying how he felt.
卡特在表达他真实的心情。

00:29:35.359 --> 00:29:39.880
He wanted to do everything Ted said in that speech is where Carter wanted to be.
他想做特德在那段独白里说的所有事，那就是卡特渴望去的地方。

00:29:39.880 --> 00:29:47.839
It was his same aspiration, and it's very vulnerable to write that, and you just performed it with such vulnerability, but also this kind of heroism.
那是他同样的追求，写下这些需要极大的勇气，而你表演时不仅展现了这种脆弱感，还有一种英雄主义色彩。

00:29:47.839 --> 00:30:00.880
You forget all about how much Ted fucked up two seconds ago, saying I love you up in the apartment, because now he's your hero, and he's so emotionally articulate and intelligent in that speech that I think he wins you over and you don't get that speech unless Ted fucked up upstairs.
你完全忘了两秒钟前特德在公寓里说“我爱你”时把事情搞砸得有多离谱，因为现在他成了你的英雄，他在那段独白中展现的情感表达力和智慧如此之高，我觉得他彻底赢得了你的心，而且如果没有特德在楼上把事情搞砸，你就听不到那段独白。

00:30:00.880 --> 00:30:11.960
So Ted has to be the guy who can fuck up upstairs and has to be the guy that absolutely sweeps you off your feet by acknowledging the fuck up and then surprising you with this unbelievable sentiment that you did not see coming in that moment.
所以特德必须得是那个在楼上把事情搞砸的人，也必须得是那个通过承认错误彻底征服你，然后用你当时始料未及的不可思议的情感震撼你的人。

00:30:11.960 --> 00:30:13.559
So I mean, it works.
所以我的意思是，这行得通。

00:30:13.559 --> 00:30:14.400
Yeah.
对。

00:30:14.400 --> 00:30:17.119
It works so well because Robin gave him the look.
效果太好了，因为罗宾给了他那个眼神。

00:30:17.119 --> 00:30:18.880
She gave him the look.
她给了他那个眼神。

00:30:18.880 --> 00:30:28.750
And this old man, he must have me I fell in love with you, New York City.
而这个老头，他一定觉得我爱上了你，纽约城。

00:30:28.750 --> 00:30:32.359
And now, commercials!
现在，插播广告！

00:30:32.359 --> 00:30:36.400
Hi, I'm Alex Goldman, host of the HyperFixed Podcast.
嗨，我是亚历克斯·戈尔德曼，“HyperFixed”播客的主持人。

00:30:36.400 --> 00:30:40.079
Each week, we take listeners' problems and try to solve them for them.
每周，我们都会收集听众的问题并尝试为他们解决。

00:30:40.079 --> 00:30:43.079
Problems like, I'm 30 and I'm scared to drive in New York.
比如这样的问题：我30岁了，但在纽约开车很害怕。

00:30:43.079 --> 00:30:46.759
Or, why can't I adjust the volume of my car stereo when I'm in reverse?
或者，为什么我倒车时不能调节车载音响的音量？

00:30:46.759 --> 00:30:49.319
We also solve non-car related problems.
我们也解决与汽车无关的问题。

00:30:49.319 --> 00:30:55.369
If you have a problem, not only will we fix it, we'll expose the hidden systems that to cause that problem in the first place.
如果你有问题，我们不仅会帮你解决，还会揭示导致该问题的隐藏系统。

00:30:55.369 --> 00:30:57.849
That's the HyperFixed Podcast from Radio Topia.
这就是来自 Radio Topia 的“HyperFixed”播客。

00:30:57.849 --> 00:31:12.450
Find it wherever you find podcasts or at HyperFixedPod.com.
你可以在各大播客平台或访问 HyperFixedPod.com 找到它。

00:31:12.450 --> 00:31:15.369
End of commercials back to show.
广告结束，回到节目。

00:31:15.369 --> 00:31:20.460
Can I ask both of you how present were you at the time?
我可以问你们两个，当时你们的代入感有多强？

00:31:20.859 --> 00:31:32.950
I mean, Craig, you would describe to me all of a sudden you go from writer to owner of rather large small business with millions of dollars on the line and you're going all over.
我是说，克雷格，你曾向我描述过，你突然从一个编剧变成了一家规模不小的公司的老板，几百万美元的资金压在身上，到处奔波。

00:31:32.950 --> 00:31:34.910
were you able to appreciate this in the moment?
你们当时能好好享受这个时刻吗？

00:31:34.910 --> 00:31:37.150
Could you breathe through it and go look what we're doing here?
你们能喘口气，然后说，看看我们在这里做的事吗？

00:31:37.150 --> 00:31:40.269
I think I did because Alec, we didn't know if the show was that, we thought that was it.
我想我是可以的，因为亚历克斯，我们不知道这部剧究竟会怎么样，我们以为也就这样了。

00:31:40.269 --> 00:31:43.430
We were shooting a pilot and we were like, I can't believe we got it this far.
我们在拍试播集，当时觉得，真不敢相信我们能走到这一步。

00:31:43.430 --> 00:31:45.309
We'll be able to show this to our friends.
我们能把这个放给朋友们看了。

00:31:45.309 --> 00:31:52.670
I mean, I did have this feeling that something was magical about like a moment like watching Josh chew that speech on that location chewed outside on the New York Street.
我是说，我确实有这种感觉，觉得有些事情非常神奇，比如看着乔什在那个外景地、在纽约街头外景地深情演绎那段独白的时刻。

00:31:52.670 --> 00:31:55.349
Like I had the feeling like this is gonna go.
我就有种感觉，这剧要火。

00:31:55.349 --> 00:32:00.069
But you don't know, it was just like, let's make this one good episode and we'll see if it ever goes in there.
但你并不知道，当时的想法就是，先把这一集拍好，看看以后有没有机会播出。

00:32:00.069 --> 00:32:02.990
probably it won't, the odds are against you, way against you.
可能不会播，几率对你不利，大大不利。

00:32:02.990 --> 00:32:06.150
So I didn't yet feel that we're running a big business.
所以我当时并没有觉得我们在经营一家大公司。

00:32:06.150 --> 00:32:14.230
I was like, we have this amount of money and this number of days and these actors, let's make the best 22-minute show we can.
我的想法是，我们有这么多预算、这么多天时间和这些演员，我们要拍出最棒的22分钟节目。

00:32:14.230 --> 00:32:16.029
And it felt very fleeting.
而且感觉非常短暂。

00:32:16.029 --> 00:32:17.470
And maybe that was part of its magic.
也许这也是它的神奇之处吧。

00:32:17.470 --> 00:32:19.150
It felt absolutely fleeting.
感觉绝对是转瞬即逝的。

00:32:19.150 --> 00:32:27.710
In that way, it felt very innocent because you look back now and you're like, well, of course, we know now they're gonna do 208 episodes of this 207 more.
从这个角度来看，它显得非常单纯，因为现在回想起来，你会觉得，好吧，当然了，我们现在知道他们会拍208集，还要再拍207集。

00:32:27.710 --> 00:32:32.190
But at the time, we were just trying to make a good show that would get the attention of the network and that would put us on the air.
但在当时，我们只是想做好一集节目，吸引电视台的注意，好让我们能登上荧屏。

00:32:32.190 --> 00:32:33.349
One good episode, yeah.
做好一集，对吧。

00:32:33.349 --> 00:32:34.549
One good episode, right?
做好一集，对吗？

00:32:34.549 --> 00:32:35.390
One good one.
做好精彩的一集。

00:32:35.390 --> 00:32:40.430
And then we, and another thing I loved watching it, just to tick off some more things that jumped out of me watching it.
然后我们，还有一件事我在观看时很喜欢，仅仅是为了挑出一些看的时候触动到我的东西。

00:32:40.430 --> 00:32:43.750
And then I'm dying to hear more about what it was like watching with Jordana, I really am.
然后我特别想听听你和乔丹娜一起看时的感受，真的特别想。

00:32:43.750 --> 00:32:45.589
I know we were saving that made from the end.
我知道我们之前说把这个留到最后。

00:32:45.589 --> 00:32:46.990
Just, I'm just so curious.
我就是，我只是太好奇了。

00:32:48.349 --> 00:32:51.710
But you know what, I loved how everything was important.
但你知道吗，我很喜欢这里面所有事情都很重要这一点。

00:32:51.710 --> 00:32:53.230
Everything was important right away.
每件事在一开始都很重要。

00:32:53.230 --> 00:32:57.190
The whole episode begins with Marshall changed my whole world.
整集开场就是马修改变了我的整个世界。

00:32:57.190 --> 00:33:00.198
He changed the game, he screwed everything up getting engaged.
他改变了游戏规则，他订婚把一切都搞砸了。

00:33:00.198 --> 00:33:01.071
That's a big deal.
这可是件大事。

00:33:01.071 --> 00:33:02.478
Your best friend got engaged.
你最好的朋友订婚了。

00:33:02.478 --> 00:33:05.050
So Ted decides I'm going to meet the love of my life.
所以泰德决定我要去寻找我一生的挚爱。

00:33:05.050 --> 00:33:07.670
And all these important things start happening, right?
所有这些重要的事情开始发生了，对吧？

00:33:07.670 --> 00:33:10.047
And all these, like everything feels high stakes.
而且所有这些，就像每件事都让人感觉事关重大。

00:33:10.047 --> 00:33:11.843
It all takes place in this one night.
这一切都发生在同一个晚上。

00:33:11.843 --> 00:33:15.142
I love all the time jumps, but the stakes of the show are one night.
我很喜欢所有的闪回和时间跳跃，但这部剧的核心 stakes 只是一个晚上。

00:33:15.142 --> 00:33:21.401
And I love that in the background of this and raising the stakes in this weird kind of stealthy way that people might not notice.
我喜欢在这个背景下，以一种人们可能不会注意到的、奇妙而隐秘的方式提升紧张感。

00:33:21.401 --> 00:33:25.428
There's a guy on a bridge who's going to jump off the bridge in Robin's news story.
在罗宾的新闻报道里，桥上有一个人准备跳桥。

00:33:25.428 --> 00:33:56.869
And I think it's so important to the pilot that there's this life or death tension just ticking this little clock ticking in the background is that guy gonna jump off that bridge and how that sort of factors back into like the moment Ted you know the the moment in the bar where they want they see that he didn't jump and Marshall says he didn't jump which first of all I felt relief I was like oh thank god that guy didn't jump right guys just like back into it and it's just like that idea of these high stakes and it's all life or death this is our life this is our life right now tonight can is going to be a story Are we gonna be tall forever?
我觉得这对于试播集来说太重要了，这种生死攸关的紧张感就像背景里的一个小钟在滴答作响，那个人会从桥上跳下去吗，这后来又是如何融入到那个时刻中的——你知道的，就是在酒吧里他们看到他没有跳，马修说他没有跳，首先这让我松了一口气，我当时想，哦谢天神那家伙没跳，对吧，大家又回到了状态，这种高风险的感觉就像这一切都是生死攸关，这就是我们的生活，这就是我们眼下的生活，今晚会不会成为一个故事，我们会永远身材高大吗？

00:33:56.869 --> 00:33:57.710
Are we gonna live?
我们会活下去吗？

00:33:57.710 --> 00:33:58.549
Are we gonna die?
我们会死吗？

00:33:58.549 --> 00:33:59.390
Are we gonna take the leap?
我们要放手一搏吗？

00:33:59.390 --> 00:34:00.230
Are we gonna not take the leap?
我们不放手一搏吗？

00:34:00.230 --> 00:34:01.750
Is taking the leap good or bad?
放手一搏是好还是坏？

00:34:01.750 --> 00:34:03.430
Ted is a great joke about that.
泰德对此开了一个很好的玩笑。

00:34:03.430 --> 00:34:09.230
And, but boy, I just, I loved how everything felt like the most important thing in the world in that part.
但是天哪，我就是，我很喜欢在那部分里，每件事感觉都像是世界上最重要的事情。

00:34:09.230 --> 00:34:12.309
What is it in the van that pulls up is we gotta jumper.
面包车停下来说的是我们有个跳桥的。

00:34:12.309 --> 00:34:13.550
We gotta jumper.
我们有个跳桥的。

00:34:13.550 --> 00:34:17.110
Yeah, that was the one where I was like, does the news van just pull up in front of our apartment?
对，那个桥段让我觉得，新闻采访车怎么就直接停在我们公寓门口了？

00:34:17.110 --> 00:34:18.190
There you are.
你们在那儿。

00:34:18.190 --> 00:34:20.949
We've been looking for you.
我们一直在找你们。

00:34:20.949 --> 00:34:22.030
Where have they been looking?
他们一直在哪儿找？

00:34:22.030 --> 00:34:24.309
Where have they come to the restaurant?
他们怎么来餐馆了？

00:34:24.309 --> 00:34:27.349
How did they know that there weren't trackers on our phones yet?
他们怎么知道那时候我们的手机上还没有定位追踪器？

00:34:27.349 --> 00:34:31.750
Greg, you pointed out that you didn't know if there would be more than one episode.
格雷格，你指出过你不知道会不会有不止一集。

00:34:31.750 --> 00:34:32.750
This was what you were doing.
这就是你当时在做的事。

00:34:32.750 --> 00:34:33.750
You sort of put your all into it.
你基本上把全部心血都倾注进去了。

00:34:33.750 --> 00:34:41.269
I think nothing that shows out in this pilot very much show is yes, it launches a series the way a great pilot launches.
我认为这部试播集非常出色的一点在于，它确实像一部优秀的试播集那样开启了一个系列。

00:34:41.269 --> 00:34:44.750
It's also just a great romantic comedy in 22 minutes.
它同时也是一部在22分钟内讲完的伟大浪漫喜剧。

00:34:44.750 --> 00:34:54.269
You kind of have a whole movie happening in here and like you're saying because everything is so important, it has that cinematic feel and it's got that story going on.
你在这里面几乎看完了一整部电影，正如你所说，因为每件事都如此重要，它有一种电影般的质感，并且故事一直在推进。

00:34:54.269 --> 00:34:58.308
the background and it has this romantic lead going through quite a bit.
背景里一直在进行着那个故事，而且浪漫的主角经历了不少波折。

00:34:58.308 --> 00:35:03.825
You just happen to end this romantic story with not getting the girl instead of getting the girl.
只是这个浪漫故事的结局恰好是没有追到女孩，而不是追到了女孩。

00:35:03.825 --> 00:35:04.325
Yeah.
对。

00:35:04.110 --> 00:35:08.034
But it really felt like you piled in a full tail in here.
但它真的感觉你在里面塞进了一个完整的故事。

00:35:08.034 --> 00:35:11.409
I think that's why it gets a lot done in a hurry.
我想这就是为什么它能在短时间内完成那么多内容。

00:35:11.409 --> 00:35:13.474
It gets a lot done in a hurry.
它在短时间内完成了很多内容。

00:35:13.474 --> 00:35:15.196
I was also proud of that.
我也为这一点感到自豪。

00:35:15.196 --> 00:35:20.705
By the end of act one, I made a little note to myself like so much has happened.
到第一幕结束时，我给自己做了一个小笔记，发生了太多的事。

00:35:20.705 --> 00:35:22.908
So much has happened in act one.
第一幕里发生了太多的事。

00:35:22.908 --> 00:35:27.659
It's like you You know so much very quickly and so much has happened.
就像你 很快就知道了那么多信息，而且发生了那么多事。

00:35:27.659 --> 00:35:28.940
I also loved another little moment.
我也很喜欢另一个小细节。

00:35:28.940 --> 00:35:40.699
I love that Robin's friend in the corner going, what's taking so long, you know, calling out because the show kind of like, it's just a hilarious thing to look back at and go, little did we know how it's going to be 208 episodes.
我喜欢罗宾角落里的朋友说，怎么花了这么长时间，你知道的，大声抱怨，因为这部剧有点像，回过头来看会觉得这太搞笑了，当时哪里知道它后来会有208集。

00:35:40.699 --> 00:35:43.019
That woman though, that was the truest line in the whole pilot.
不过那个女人说的，那是整集试播集里最真实的一句台词。

00:35:43.019 --> 00:35:50.780
It was the woman who goes, what's taking so long, because the whole thing was going to take longer than anyone ever thought and we brought her back and you're the end of the show.
就是那个问“还要多久”的女人，因为整件事花的时间比任何人预想的都要长，我们把她带了回来，而你就是节目的结尾。

00:35:50.780 --> 00:35:53.380
I love the what's taking so long, girl.
我太喜欢那个问“还要多久”的女孩了。

00:35:53.380 --> 00:35:57.579
It's sort of called it just sort of became like the theme of the show, what's taking so long?
这可以说是成为了整部剧的主题，还要多久？

00:35:57.579 --> 00:35:58.579
Well, we're going to take that fucking year.
好吧，我们他妈的就要花上整整一年。

00:35:58.579 --> 00:35:59.579
It's how long is it?
这到底是多久？

00:35:59.579 --> 00:36:00.579
Robyn and Ted are talking.
罗宾和泰德在说话。

00:36:00.579 --> 00:36:04.619
Robyn and Ted are talking and she wants to, yeah, the friend wants her to come back to the corner.
罗宾和泰德在说话，她想让——对，朋友想让她回到角落里。

00:36:04.619 --> 00:36:05.619
What's taking so long?
还要多久？

00:36:05.619 --> 00:36:06.619
Because she's had a break up.
因为她刚分手。

00:36:06.619 --> 00:36:07.619
He's had a break up and she's mad.
他也刚分手，而且她很生气。

00:36:07.619 --> 00:36:08.619
She hates all of that.
她讨厌所有这一切。

00:36:08.619 --> 00:36:09.619
Yeah, she hates all of that.
对，她讨厌所有这一切。

00:36:09.619 --> 00:36:13.420
And she wants, she wants, she's like, where's my next round where I need to get drunker than this.
她想要的是，她觉得，我的下一轮在哪里，我需要喝得比这更醉。

00:36:13.420 --> 00:36:14.420
I just got dumped.
我刚被甩了。

00:36:14.420 --> 00:36:20.820
And that exchange, that also was so great, but you and the other moment I thought, I mean many moments I thought, hey, this might go somewhere.
而那段对话，同样也太棒了，但你还有另一个时刻——我觉得，或者说有很多时刻——让我觉得，嘿，这说不定能成。

00:36:20.820 --> 00:36:31.500
You and Robin up at that bar, Ted and Robin up at that bar, and the whole thing that ends with the drink in the face, which was one of the audition scenes that got both of you cast because you were magic even in an audition.
你和罗宾在那个酒吧里，泰德和罗宾在那个酒吧里，以脸上被泼了一杯酒结束的那一幕，那是让你们俩都通过试镜的场景之一，因为即使在试镜时你们也充满了魔力。

00:36:31.500 --> 00:36:35.059
That was another moment where I was like, I remember feeling pretty good in that scene.
那是另一个让我觉得——我记得在那场戏里感觉挺好的。

00:36:35.059 --> 00:36:37.780
I mean, all over the place, I was feeling pretty good about it.
我是说，在各个地方，我都觉得感觉挺不错。

00:36:37.780 --> 00:36:44.260
Do you remember also, it was the very last thing we shot, it was with Ranjit around the table at the bar, the funny reveal that Ranjit is with us?
你还记得吗，那也是我们拍的最后一个镜头，是和兰吉特一起在酒吧的桌子旁，那个有趣的揭秘——兰吉特和我们在一起？

00:36:44.260 --> 00:36:48.539
That lean in, oh my God, signal, Ranjit, what he's so good.
那个前倾的动作，天哪，暗号，兰吉特，他太棒了。

00:36:48.539 --> 00:36:49.539
He's so good.
他太棒了。

00:36:49.539 --> 00:36:53.420
Oh, good, Marshall Manhand, he ended up doing, what, 20 episodes?
哦，太好了，马歇尔一掌抓过去，他最后拍了多少集，20集？

00:36:53.420 --> 00:36:54.420
He did a bunch.
他拍了不少。

00:36:54.420 --> 00:36:59.059
Yeah, he was an important, he was, oh man, him, him also kind of shutting out of your signal.
对，他是个重要人物，他是个，天哪，他，他甚至还打断了你的暗号。

00:36:59.059 --> 00:37:00.059
That was a signal.
那是一个暗号。

00:37:00.059 --> 00:37:01.059
Yeah.
对。

00:37:01.059 --> 00:37:06.679
And I also remember they gave us real champagne to one hell of a night, to one hell of a hell of a night.
而且我还记得他们给了我们真正的香槟，为了一个了不起的夜晚，为了一个绝对了不起的夜晚。

00:37:06.679 --> 00:37:10.500
Yeah, they gave us real champagne, which is not something I've done a lot on the set.
对，他们给了我们真正的香槟，这可不是我在片场经常经历的事。

00:37:10.500 --> 00:37:15.860
But it was, it was the last night of the shooting of the pilot and we all had a little bit of champagne.
但那是——那是试播集拍摄的最后一晚，我们都喝了一点香槟。

00:37:15.860 --> 00:37:19.460
There was just such a warm, good feeling on set, like I think we made something good.
片场有一种非常温暖、美好的感觉，就像我觉得我们拍出了一部好作品。

00:37:19.460 --> 00:37:21.139
That's what it felt like, right?
感觉就是这样，对吧？

00:37:21.139 --> 00:37:22.659
It felt like we had made something good.
感觉我们拍出了一部好作品。

00:37:22.659 --> 00:37:25.139
It felt like we had made the thing we set out to make.
感觉我们拍出了我们最初打算拍的东西。

00:37:25.139 --> 00:37:26.539
It really felt that way.
真的有这种感觉。

00:37:26.539 --> 00:37:32.179
Like any endeavor in life, especially a TV pilot too, it doesn't always feel that way.
就像生活中的任何努力一样，尤其是电视试播集，感觉并不总是这样。

00:37:32.179 --> 00:37:35.139
And it had this magic, there was just a magic to it.
它有一种魔力，其中就是有一种魔力。

00:37:35.139 --> 00:37:40.539
I just felt like a magic and a life to it and something that was bigger than the sum of its parts and bigger than me or you or anything.
我只觉得它有一种魔力、一种生命力，某种大于其各部分总和、大于我或你或任何东西的东西。

00:37:40.539 --> 00:37:45.619
And it was like, I felt so much gratitude watching it and so much gratitude to the fans.
当时我就觉得，在观看它时充满了感激之情，对粉丝们也充满了感激之情。

00:37:45.619 --> 00:37:50.179
Because people found that it was a weird, It was a weird show for CBS.
因为人们发现它是一部奇怪的剧，对于CBS来说是一部奇怪的剧。

00:37:50.179 --> 00:37:51.460
With a weird title.
名字也很奇怪。

00:37:51.460 --> 00:37:52.500
Weird title.
奇怪的名字。

00:37:52.500 --> 00:38:03.780
Oh, and I mean, we tested that pilot and we're told, so after, you have this magic time shooting the pilot and then you take it and you take it to this focus groups and you put it through this somewhat corporate testing process which every pilot does.
哦，我是说，我们对那集试播集进行了测试，然后被告知——所以在你经历了一段神奇的试播集拍摄时光之后，你把它拿去给焦点小组观看，让它经历这种某种程度上的公司测试流程，每一部试播集都要经历这个。

00:38:04.820 --> 00:38:09.139
And one of the takeaways from the testing was people hate the title.
测试的结论之一就是人们讨厌这个标题。

00:38:09.139 --> 00:38:13.860
It was like, I remember the testing people being like, everyone's confused by this.
就像，我记得测试人员说，每个人都被这个搞糊涂了。

00:38:13.860 --> 00:38:16.420
You have to, I've strong recommendation to change this.
你们必须——我强烈建议改掉这个。

00:38:16.420 --> 00:38:22.860
And they were saying in front of the studio executives I was like, don't shut up, don't do this, can we just talk over here away from them?
他们当时当着高管的面说这话，我就想：闭嘴，别这样，我们能不能去那边离他们远点的地方谈谈？

00:38:22.860 --> 00:38:24.300
Because we did not want to change the title.
因为我们根本不想改标题。

00:38:24.300 --> 00:38:27.739
The title is the thing, thank God, again, the studio and the network.
标题就是关键所在，谢天谢地，还是那句话，制片厂和电视台。

00:38:27.739 --> 00:38:30.940
They believed in the show, they knew the title, sold the show.
他们对这部剧坚信不疑，他们知道这个标题能卖出这部剧。

00:38:30.940 --> 00:38:31.940
And so we didn't.
所以我们没有改。

00:38:31.940 --> 00:38:33.500
But there were discussions.
但当时确实有过讨论。

00:38:33.500 --> 00:38:49.300
Is a title to confusing those time jumps I talked about, boom, boom, boom, what, what, sure enough, some people in the focus group that were, that it accepted $50 to watch a pilot last Sunday afternoon in the valley, you would wonder it in from the mall, next door or whatever.
标题会不会太令人困惑了，我提到的那些时间跳跃，轰、轰、轰，什么，什么，果不其然，焦点小组里有些人，他们是上周日下午在山谷里拿了50美元来看试播集的，你可能会觉得他们是从隔壁商场什么的闲逛过来的。

00:38:49.300 --> 00:38:50.619
They were like, I don't get it.
他们就说，我看不懂。

00:38:50.619 --> 00:38:51.659
What's that's weird?
那是什么，好奇怪？

00:38:51.659 --> 00:38:54.300
And if you were asked, did that confuse you?
如果你被问到，这有让你感到困惑吗？

00:38:54.300 --> 00:38:56.340
And you go, yeah, that is confusing.
你就会说，对，这确实很困惑。

00:38:56.340 --> 00:38:58.699
What a jump to the future, what happened?
怎么一下子跳到未来了，发生了什么？

00:38:58.699 --> 00:39:07.460
And people, so it's very nerve-wracking when you put something this intricate and unique through that process, and you're just terrified.
所以人们，当你把如此复杂且独特的东西送进这个流程时，会让人非常伤脑筋，你简直吓坏了。

00:39:07.460 --> 00:39:10.219
It's going to come out the worst, but it didn't.
生怕它出来之后效果最差，但并没有。

00:39:10.219 --> 00:39:11.300
It really didn't.
真的没有。

00:39:11.300 --> 00:39:25.920
When you were talking about how much had already happened by the end of Act One, I feel like because of the narrative structure, it's such a plot heavy show, such a propulsive show, so it doesn't, it can't withstand a lot of fat on it.
当你谈到第一幕结束时已经发生了多少事情时，我觉得因为叙事结构的缘故，这是一部情节非常密集的剧，一部极具推进力的剧，所以它经不起很多臃肿的废话。

00:39:25.920 --> 00:39:44.179
It's like everything was trimmed down to its essence, which is astonishing because my memory of doing the show while the plot is delightful and strange and always surprising, you still get so many good jokes in there, but they're character revealing and they're a plot forwarding all the dress.
就像每一样东西都被修剪到了它的精髓，这太惊人了，因为我对拍这部剧的记忆是，虽然剧情既美妙、诡异又总是充满惊喜，你依然能在其中听到那么多精彩的笑话，但它们既能展现角色，又能推动剧情发展，全都在那里。

00:39:44.179 --> 00:39:45.420
They're one or the other or both.
它们要么是其中之一，要么两者兼具。

00:39:45.420 --> 00:39:46.260
That's exactly it.
完全就是这样。

00:39:46.260 --> 00:39:48.460
They're character revealing or plot forwarding or both.
它们要么是展现角色，要么是推动剧情，或者两者兼具。

00:39:48.460 --> 00:39:50.139
There's almost nothing that isn't that.
几乎没有哪个笑话不具备这些功能。

00:39:50.139 --> 00:39:53.179
And by the way, that didn't start that way.
顺便说一句，一开始并不是这样的。

00:39:53.179 --> 00:39:54.579
That cut of that pilot came in.
那集试播集的初剪版交上来时。

00:39:54.579 --> 00:39:55.659
It's supposed to be 22 minutes.
本来应该是22分钟。

00:39:55.659 --> 00:39:57.619
I think it was 31 minutes.
我记得当时有31分钟。

00:39:57.619 --> 00:40:00.780
Overshot, we cut out whole scenes, all this fat.
拍得太长了，我们剪掉了整场的戏，所有这些废话。

00:40:00.780 --> 00:40:03.579
We learned so much editing that show.
我们在剪辑这部剧的过程中学到了太多。

00:40:03.579 --> 00:40:05.659
It really was built in the editor room in a lot of ways.
在很大程度上，它确实是在剪辑室里完成的。

00:40:05.659 --> 00:40:12.219
And when you see the edited version and you're just heartbroken most of the time because you're like, oh that's gone.
而当你看到剪辑版时，你大部分时间都会感到心碎，因为你会觉得，哦，那个没了。

00:40:12.219 --> 00:40:12.787
love that.
我太喜欢那个了。

00:40:12.787 --> 00:40:13.808
Where did that go?
那个去哪儿了？

00:40:13.808 --> 00:40:15.226
No, this just got better.
不，这样反而更好了。

00:40:15.226 --> 00:40:16.417
This just got better.
这样反而更好了。

00:40:16.417 --> 00:40:19.026
Everything we cut out, we should have cut out.
我们剪掉的一切，本来就该剪掉。

00:40:19.026 --> 00:40:19.526
Yeah.
对。

00:40:19.526 --> 00:40:20.150
Everything.
一切。

00:40:20.150 --> 00:40:20.717
Oh my God.
天哪。

00:40:20.717 --> 00:40:21.965
It was so overwritten.
台词写得太多了。

00:40:21.965 --> 00:40:22.986
It came in so fat.
交上来的时候太臃肿了。

00:40:22.986 --> 00:40:24.404
It wasn't great at first.
一开始并不是那么完美。

00:40:24.404 --> 00:40:26.672
And we whittled it down on what you did.
我们把它一点点削减成了你现在看到的这样。

