第一，橄榄理论是从哪来的？

00:40:29.831 --> 00:40:37.115
I think that was that I think that was also a Carter Bayes gem because but but it's funny because it was sort of a Marshall and Lily thing.
我想那是——我想那也是卡特·贝斯（Carter Bays）的绝妙点子，不过很有趣，因为它有点像是马歇尔和莉莉的事。

00:40:37.115 --> 00:40:45.762
It might have been, might have been Carter's observation of like, you know, being close friends with me and seeing maybe this old married guy at age, you know, 2021.
也有可能，可能是卡特观察到——你知道，作为我的密友，看到我这样一个在20、21岁时就已经像个老夫老妻一样的人。

00:40:45.762 --> 00:40:58.653
I mean, basically, and then even more so by the time I was 30 and like the negotiations of couple, I think it was Carter's like stealth commentary on like, you know, watching a guy in a relationship like bend a little bit and choose your battles.
我是说，基本上是这样，甚至到我30岁时更是如此，比如情侣之间的妥协，我想这是卡特的一种潜台词，你知道的，看着一个处于恋爱中的男人做出一点妥协，懂得挑选值得争论的事情。

00:40:58.653 --> 00:41:02.583
And I don't know why I wouldn't want to dissuade this person of this thing.
我不知道为什么我不想打消这个人对这件事的坚持。

00:41:02.583 --> 00:41:11.544
And like, I think it was, I think it was Carter's clever, Clever commentary slash slight dis on maybe my my close friend watching me in a relationship with a strong woman.
而且，我觉得那是——我觉得那是卡特既聪明又巧妙的评论，顺便也稍微吐槽了一下我——我的密友看着我和一个强势的女人谈恋爱。

00:41:11.544 --> 00:41:13.011
I was like, I don't want it.
我当时就想，我才不要。

00:41:13.011 --> 00:41:17.779
I think it was like, I think it was a little bit fat, which I don't think that had any day.
我觉得这有点——我觉得那没有任何问题。

00:41:18.820 --> 00:41:29.355
The only thing in the pilot that I found that made me cringe and I think would probably, you would not write today is Barney's opening lines.
试播集里唯一让我觉得尴尬、而且我觉得你现在绝对不会写出来的东西，就是巴尼的开场白。

00:41:29.355 --> 00:41:29.855
Right.
对。

00:41:29.855 --> 00:41:30.355
Yeah.
对。

00:41:30.355 --> 00:41:30.855
Yeah.
对。

00:41:30.855 --> 00:41:38.924
It's like he's talking about like, Yeah, what type of women he likes, and I used to like this kind of thing.
就好像他在谈论，对，他喜欢什么类型的女性，而我以前也喜欢这种类型。

00:41:38.619 --> 00:41:45.659
Yeah, it feels a little insensitive, and obviously that's kind of who Barney is.
是的，感觉有点不够体贴，当然这正是巴尼的性格。

00:41:45.659 --> 00:41:48.509
It's kind of, I thought the same thing.
某种程度上，我也这么觉得。

00:41:48.509 --> 00:41:55.304
I felt a little bit not proud of that, but I felt like that was our opening thing for Barney.
我对此其实有点说不上骄傲，但我觉得这就是我们为巴尼设定的开场形象。

00:41:55.304 --> 00:41:56.619
What are we doing?
我们在干什么？

00:41:56.619 --> 00:41:57.563
What are we doing?
我们在干什么？

00:41:57.563 --> 00:42:07.420
It's a tricky thing, and this is something we'll probably unpack about that characters we go, but it's a tricky thing where it's like, you never were actually endorsing Barney's worldview.
这是一个棘手的问题，这也是我们以后可能会剖析这些角色时会谈到的，这是一个棘手的地方，因为你从来没有真正赞同过巴尼的世界观。

00:42:07.420 --> 00:42:24.860
You were trying to write a character that had these very strange kind of out there ideas and schemes and all this stuff, but it's a tricky thing when you're writing a character like that who's held up to not be like emulated, but to be slightly kind of mocked or roll your eyes at them.
你当时是想塑造一个角色，他有一些非常奇怪、天马行空的想法和诡计之类的事情，但当你塑造这样一个角色时，这就变得很棘手了——他不是那种被效仿的对象，而是那种稍微有点被嘲笑或让人翻白眼的对象。

00:42:24.860 --> 00:42:35.897
But then you get people, it's kind of like Michael Douglas in Wall Street, you know, Oliver Stone, who's as lefty of filmmakers you could get, created a character that like finance Bros.
但后来你会发现有些人，这就有点像《华尔街》里的迈克尔·道格拉斯，你知道，奥利弗·斯通作为一个极左翼的电影人，塑造了一个让金融界精英……

00:42:35.897 --> 00:42:39.219
and college like had Gordon Gecko posters on their wall.
和大学生们把戈登·盖柯的海报贴在墙上的角色。

00:42:39.219 --> 00:42:40.219
I know.
我知道。

00:42:40.219 --> 00:42:41.219
He is good.
他演得真好。

00:42:41.219 --> 00:42:42.219
He is good.
他演得真好。

00:42:42.219 --> 00:42:43.219
And they started it.
他们就开始了。

00:42:43.219 --> 00:42:46.260
It's like they missed the entire condemnation, right?
就好像他们完全没有get到那种批判一样，对吧？

00:42:46.260 --> 00:42:47.260
Right.
对。

00:42:47.260 --> 00:42:48.260
Right.
对。

00:42:48.260 --> 00:42:49.260
And that happens with Barney.
巴尼身上也是这样。

00:42:49.260 --> 00:43:00.447
You know, Barney, we imagine Barney as a parody of toxic masculinity, and underneath it, there's this very needy human who wants Ted to put on a suit, play Laser Tag and be is best friend.
你知道的，巴尼，我们把巴尼设想为对有毒男子气概的一种讽刺，而在它的内核之下，其实是一个非常缺乏安全感、渴望让泰德穿上西装、玩激光枪战并成为他最好朋友的人。

00:43:00.447 --> 00:43:02.471
That's what I loved in this pilot.
这正是我喜欢这部试播集的地方。

00:43:02.471 --> 00:43:05.803
It reminded me of like the original intention of Barney.
它让我想起了巴尼最初的设定意图。

00:43:05.803 --> 00:43:13.004
I was watching the pilot and I thought to myself, Barney on Saturday night is playing laser tag with children by himself.
我当时在看试播集时就在想，周六晚上的巴尼竟然自己一个人和小孩子们在玩激光枪战。

00:43:13.004 --> 00:43:14.254
Yeah, it's quite sad.
是的，这挺让人心酸的。

00:43:14.254 --> 00:43:19.074
It's sad in a way and it's like he really wanted Ted to come and Ted didn't come.
从某种程度上说这很悲伤，就好像他真的很希望泰德来，但泰德没有来。

00:43:19.074 --> 00:43:25.679
And he's pretending he's like, laser tag is so awesome, but he abandons laser tag immediately to come join Ted.
而且他还在假装，说什么激光枪战太棒了，但一看到泰德加入，他马上就把激光枪战抛在脑后了。

00:43:25.679 --> 00:43:29.679
And all he wants is for Ted to put on his suit and be reassured he's his best friend.
他想要的只是让泰德穿上西装，并确信自己是泰德最好的朋友。

00:43:29.679 --> 00:43:31.000
And that is the essence of Barney.
而这正是巴尼的精髓所在。

00:43:31.000 --> 00:43:36.360
I was proud of that because sometimes we do, sometimes people watch Barney and they think he's to be emulated.
我为这一点感到骄傲，因为有时我们会发现，有时人们看巴尼时会觉得他是值得效仿的对象。

00:43:36.360 --> 00:43:40.269
Sometimes people watch Barney and they go, you couldn't get away with that now.
而有时人们看巴尼时会说，你现在可不能这么干了。

00:43:40.269 --> 00:43:47.030
And I guess my response to that for me, and it doesn't mean we always hit the target here, but for me, we were never trying to get away with that.
我想我对这一点当下的回应——这并不意味着我们每次都切中靶心——但对我来说，我们从来没有想过要“蒙混过关”。

00:43:47.030 --> 00:43:50.510
We were always trying to be, to make a caricature and a parody.
我们一直试图去刻画一个讽刺人物和一个滑稽角色。

00:43:50.510 --> 00:43:59.395
And then even as early as a pilot, we were trying to dig underneath that bravado and that suit, and see this kind of needy human vulnerable person underneath.
甚至早在试播集中，我们就试图剖析那种虚张声势和西装革履的外表，去看到隐藏在背后的那个缺乏安全感、脆弱的人。

00:43:59.395 --> 00:44:04.850
And I could point to a lot of other episodes with Barney where we tried to do that down the line.
在后来的剧集中，我可以指出很多其他关于巴尼的片段，我们都在努力做到这一点。

00:44:04.850 --> 00:44:09.349
And I think that was the the genetic code, the DNA code of Barney and the pilot.
我认为这就是巴尼在试播集中的基因密码、DNA密码。

00:44:09.349 --> 00:44:12.723
I think the best Barney moments through the series did that.
我认为整部剧中最好的巴尼时刻都做到了这一点。

00:44:12.723 --> 00:44:20.203
And maybe the ones where we were just doing like Barney jokes that became single on Honduras or became like became sort of one level.
也许有时候我们只是在开巴尼的玩笑，导致这些玩笑变得像单口喜剧一样，或者变得有些流于表面。

00:44:20.203 --> 00:44:28.470
And maybe we did too much of that at times and it was like we weren't making the commentary about Barney, we weren't showing the person underneath.
也许有时候我们做得有点过火了，就好像我们没有对巴尼进行深度的解读，没有展现出他内心的真实人格。

00:44:28.469 --> 00:44:39.110
I'm sure we did plenty of that that probably doesn't hold up as well, but I think the moments where we were showing the human, the needy human underneath it all, that was the character.
我敢说我们确实拍了很多那样的桥段，现在看来可能经不起推敲，但我觉得那些展现了他内心深处那个富有同理心、渴望被爱的人的时刻，那才是真正的角色。

00:44:39.110 --> 00:44:40.110
That was the character.
那才是真正的角色。

00:44:40.110 --> 00:44:41.869
Neil played that so beautifully.
尼尔把这个角色演得太美妙了。

00:44:41.869 --> 00:44:47.670
You really get that in the pilot, I think, and that's what I was struck by is how late they stayed up.
我想你在试播集中真的能深深感受到这一点，这也是最让我触动的地方，那就是他们熬得太晚了。

00:44:47.670 --> 00:44:58.750
I was so tired watching it but I was like it's got to be it's like 145 like they're going to meet at the bar for last round like Jesus I'm a lot of energy.
我看的时候觉得好累，心想：天呐，这都快凌晨1点45了吧，他们还要去酒吧喝最后一轮，天啊，他们精力真好。

00:44:58.750 --> 00:44:59.750
I was so tired.
我当时太累了。

00:44:59.750 --> 00:45:04.929
I was watching it at like 953 at night and I was like I got it to start turning this off pretty soon.
我是在晚上9点53分左右看的，当时就在想，我得赶紧把它关了。

00:45:04.929 --> 00:45:08.110
I'm going to ruin my next day if I don't get to sleep.
要是不去睡觉，我明天的一整天就全毁了。

00:45:08.110 --> 00:45:15.880
Josh just to have it on the record do you remember what the first scene the first scene you shot was of this whole this whole show?
乔斯，为了留下记录，你还记得整部剧拍的第一场戏是什么吗？

00:45:15.880 --> 00:45:17.340
It might have been the first one.
可能就是第一场吧。

00:45:17.340 --> 00:45:21.019
might have been martial like the fake out that he was proposing to me.
可能是马歇尔向我求婚的那场恶作剧。

00:45:21.019 --> 00:45:22.179
That's what I think it was.
我也觉得是那场。

00:45:22.179 --> 00:45:23.420
I think it was that.
我也觉得是。

00:45:23.420 --> 00:45:25.460
I think it was Jason proposing on his bed.
我觉得就是杰森在他床上求婚那场。

00:45:25.460 --> 00:45:26.300
I think that was it.
我觉得就是那场。

00:45:26.300 --> 00:45:27.780
I think we said let's start at the beginning.
我想我们当时说的是，那就从头开始拍吧。

00:45:27.780 --> 00:45:32.019
Because that's essentially the first scene that it's sort of like a montage and photos and music and that's it.
因为那基本上就是第一场戏，里面有点像蒙太奇、照片和音乐的结合，就是这样。

00:45:32.019 --> 00:45:36.059
And I think Pam again, our director was very smart to say let's begin at the beginning and let's end at the ending.
而且我认为帕姆——我们的导演——非常聪明，她提出从头开始拍，并在结尾处结束。

00:45:36.059 --> 00:45:37.820
I'll have to jumble in between.
中间的部分可以打乱。

00:45:37.820 --> 00:45:39.539
But let's give ourselves that frame.
但我们要给自己设定这样一个框架。

00:45:39.539 --> 00:45:40.380
That is it.
就是这样。

00:45:40.380 --> 00:45:41.380
That's what it was.
当时就是这样的。

00:45:41.380 --> 00:45:42.380
And it was you and Jason together.
而且当时是你和杰森在一起。

00:45:42.380 --> 00:45:50.019
We haven't talked much about how great Jason is in the pilot and what a great best friend chemistry, you guys had immediately in that scene that sets the whole tone for the time.
我们之前没怎么聊过杰森在试播集里有多棒，以及你们俩在那个场景中立即展现出了多棒的死党化学反应，这为整部剧奠定了基调。

00:45:50.019 --> 00:45:54.659
You know, it was making me laugh was the three of them in the cab waiting downstairs.
你知道，当时让我笑出声的是他们三个在楼下等着的出租车里那一幕。

00:45:54.659 --> 00:45:56.699
There is really funny.
那段真的很好笑。

00:45:56.699 --> 00:45:59.460
And this is a really good thing to actually talk about.
其实这是一个非常值得探讨的好话题。

00:45:59.460 --> 00:46:10.179
One of the things we all talked about and agreed, the cast, you, Carter, Pam, was the idea on a sitcom, most sitcoms, people are saying hilarious stuff and no one ever laughs.
我们大家——包括演员阵容、你、卡特、帕姆——讨论并达成共识的一点是，在情景喜剧中，大多数情景喜剧里，人们都在说搞笑的话，但从来没有人会笑。

00:46:10.179 --> 00:46:15.400
But we decided as friends in a show that we wanted to have some very similar to to it.
但我们决定，作为剧中的好朋友，我们想要呈现一些非常真实自然的反应。

00:46:15.400 --> 00:46:20.500
If someone said something that was clearly meant to be a joke, we would laugh at it.
如果有人说了一句明显是笑话的话，我们会笑出来。

00:46:20.500 --> 00:46:21.701
Or she was not the last.
或者哪怕不是大笑。

00:46:21.701 --> 00:46:22.201
Yeah.
是的。

00:46:22.201 --> 00:46:22.702
But react.
而是做出反应。

00:46:22.702 --> 00:46:23.202
React.
做出反应。

00:46:23.202 --> 00:46:25.957
Barney and Marshall are really making each other laugh.
巴尼和马歇尔真的把彼此逗笑了。

00:46:25.957 --> 00:46:30.314
And I think Neil and Jason were really making each other laugh in the back of that cab.
我想尼尔和杰森在那个出租车后座上也确实把彼此逗笑了。

00:46:30.314 --> 00:46:33.119
And there's something really charming and real about it.
这里面有一种非常迷人且真实的东西。

00:46:33.119 --> 00:46:36.424
That is a great observation that carried through the whole series.
这是一个极好的观察，并且贯穿了整部剧。

00:46:36.424 --> 00:46:39.029
And I think made the show feel real or door to door.
我认为正是这一点让这部剧显得真实接地气。

00:46:38.579 --> 00:46:41.449
Because he would always cut in these reaction shots.
因为他总是会剪辑进这些反应镜头。

00:46:41.449 --> 00:46:50.113
If someone says something at the booth and you cut to them laughing and I thought it made the show kind of extra charming that we weren't oblivious to humor.
如果有人在卡座里说了什么，镜头就会切到其他人笑的画面，我觉得这让这部剧增添了一种额外的魅力，因为我们并不是对幽默无动于衷。

00:46:50.113 --> 00:46:51.768
Jason was really good at that.
杰森在这方面真的很擅长。

00:46:51.768 --> 00:46:55.962
He would be, he would be like, I mean, he was amused to being in that pilot.
他会……他会表现得，我的意思是，他很享受拍摄那部试播集。

00:46:55.962 --> 00:46:59.825
Like he was having a good time in a show and he was happy to be there.
就像他在节目里玩得很开心，并且很高兴能来到这里一样。

00:46:59.825 --> 00:47:03.467
And like, you really felt that and you felt that between you guys.
真的能感受到这一点，而且你们俩之间也能感受到这种化学反应。

00:47:03.467 --> 00:47:03.967
Yeah.
是的。

00:47:03.967 --> 00:47:06.339
And they're in them down that cab is great.
他们在那个出租车里的戏份真的很棒。

00:47:06.339 --> 00:47:10.644
Even Barney saying to Marshall when they're alone that cab, don't get married.
甚至当巴尼在出租车里单独对马歇尔说“别结婚”的时候。

00:47:10.420 --> 00:47:11.217
even that felt big.
甚至连那句话都分量十足。

00:47:11.217 --> 00:47:14.659
It's like he just got engaged with that day and Bernie's going, don't get married.
就好像他那天刚订婚，而巴尼却在一旁说，别结婚。

00:47:14.659 --> 00:47:19.380
Like there was so many, like every scene had some high stakes thing in it or two or three of them.
这里面有太多东西了，每一个场景都有一个或两三个高风险的关键冲突。

00:47:19.380 --> 00:47:19.860
Yeah.
是的。

00:47:19.860 --> 00:47:23.006
Like even that stuck jumped out of me or it's like, that's important.
甚至连那个桥段也让我印象深刻，就好像，这很重要。

00:47:23.006 --> 00:47:28.479
That's one of his close friends saying you're making a horrible mistake on the biggest day of his life proposing a lily.
这是他其中一个密友在他向莉莉求婚、人生中最重要的一天里，对他说你正在犯一个可怕的错误。

00:47:28.479 --> 00:47:29.300
It's a bold swing.
这是一个很大胆的尝试。

00:47:29.300 --> 00:47:33.699
Well, it's also like they say who you marry is the most important decision you'll ever make.
嗯，就像他们说的那样，你和谁结婚是你这辈子做出的最重要的决定。

00:47:33.699 --> 00:47:35.540
I mean, this is high stakes stuff anyway.
我的意思是，这本来就是一件人生大事。

00:47:35.540 --> 00:47:38.099
It's high stakes and Bernie is afraid of losing Marshall.
这确实是人生大事，而且巴尼害怕失去马歇尔。

00:47:38.099 --> 00:47:39.333
I'm going to lose Marshall.
我要失去马歇尔了。

00:47:39.333 --> 00:47:44.128
I'll never see Marshall and he's going to get married and go away, which again is that thing from Barney.
我再也见不到马歇尔了，他要结婚然后离开我了，这再次体现了巴尼的性格。

00:47:44.128 --> 00:47:50.523
He doesn't want to lose people because, well, we dig into Barney's childhood later in the show and we see that you grew up with that of Dad.
他不想失去身边的人，因为，嗯，我们在剧集后期深入挖掘了巴尼的童年，我们发现他是单亲家庭长大的。

00:47:50.523 --> 00:47:53.126
And, you know, he had to not have the easiest time of it.
而且，你知道的，他的成长经历肯定不容易。

00:47:53.126 --> 00:47:55.181
And I like that you sense that in that pilot.
而且我挺喜欢你在那集试播集里能感觉到这一点。

00:47:55.181 --> 00:48:06.920
Craig, can you just talk a little bit about Marshall and Lily since this is your, in some way, your marriage put up on screen that you decided to do about their story and what it was like now seeing them compared to how you were thinking about it back then?
克雷格，你能简单聊聊马歇尔和莉莉吗？因为某种程度上，这是你们的婚姻被搬上了银幕，这是你们决定以他们的故事为核心创作的，现在看他们和当年你们构思的时候相比，感觉怎么样？

00:48:06.920 --> 00:48:11.239
And I just thought Jason and Alison were so sweet and daring and wonderful.
而且我真的觉得杰森和艾莉森非常甜、非常勇敢、非常棒。

00:48:11.239 --> 00:48:24.880
It made me, it just, it just made me so happy to see, um, I liked, I liked remembering that this was a big part of the inception of the show, that they're the sort of the foundation, they're the adults, they're the parents, they're like, tell us about your date, son.
这让我觉得，这让我、这让我感到非常高兴，看到——我喜欢、我喜欢回想起这是节目最初构思的重要部分，他们是某种基础，他们是成年人，他们是父母，他们就像是，儿子，跟我们讲讲你的约会吧。

00:48:24.880 --> 00:48:29.480
There's some kind of like emotional grounding and foundation to them that they bring.
他们带来了一种情感上的支撑和基础。

00:48:29.480 --> 00:48:32.659
And Lily's more grown up than Marshall.
而且莉莉比马歇尔更成熟。

00:48:32.659 --> 00:48:35.139
Just like most women in their 20s are more grown up than men.
就像二十多岁的绝大多数女性都比男性成熟一样。

00:48:35.139 --> 00:48:37.619
my wife, fiance, then wife, was compared to me.
我当时的未婚妻、后来的妻子，也是这样被拿来和我做比较的。

00:48:37.619 --> 00:48:38.938
And I liked that dynamic.
而且我很喜欢这种动态。

00:48:38.938 --> 00:48:44.740
She was the grown-up, she comes in, she has been felt up by a kindergartener, she has a handprint on her boob.
她是那个成熟的人，她走进来，被一个幼儿园小孩摸了，胸口还有一个手印。

00:48:44.740 --> 00:48:47.431
And she just comes in and is so funny and charming.
结果她就这么走进来，还那么搞笑、那么迷人。

00:48:47.431 --> 00:48:53.392
And Jason's more all over the place in insecure and a little bit of a lost puppy dog, but he loves her so dearly.
而杰森则显得有点没谱、缺乏安全感，有点像一只迷路的小狗，但他深爱着她。

00:48:53.392 --> 00:48:58.825
And the bond and foundation that they provide, I think brings everything down to earth in that episode.
我觉得，他们所提供的羁绊和基础让那一集的所有内容都落回了实处。

00:48:58.825 --> 00:49:01.885
And they're getting engaged, they're going to get married.
而且他们订婚了，他们要结婚了。

00:49:01.885 --> 00:49:04.997
They're the promise of what the whole season's going to be.
他们就是整季走向的承诺。

00:49:04.997 --> 00:49:06.579
There's going to be a wedding.
会有一场婚礼。

00:49:06.579 --> 00:49:10.456
This is big, important stuff, and you love them right away, I think.
这些都是宏大、重要的事情，而且我觉得你一下子就会爱上他们。

00:49:10.456 --> 00:49:13.591
I'm not saying I'm such a lovable person or my wife is.
我不是说我是一个多招人喜欢的人，或者我妻子是。

00:49:13.591 --> 00:49:14.845
I like to think of it.
我愿意这么认为。

00:49:14.845 --> 00:49:16.327
I think it's very lovable.
我觉得这非常讨人喜欢。

00:49:16.327 --> 00:49:21.286
I mean, I think people, anybody that's come this far in the podcast already knows that.
我是说，我觉得大家，任何追播客追到这一步的人都已经知道了。

00:49:21.286 --> 00:49:25.961
But I really was, I was aware of how much they anchored the universe of that show.
但我真的很清楚，他们把那部剧的世界观稳稳地锚定了。

00:49:25.961 --> 00:49:36.962
They were what Ted wanted to be and the audience, they were the promise of what was going to happen in that season, they're going to get married, we're going to see a wedding, or are we, right?
他们就是泰德渴望成为的样子，对于观众来说，他们是那一季将要发生之事的承诺——他们要结婚了，我们会看到一场婚礼，或者说，真的是这样吗？

00:49:36.962 --> 00:49:39.299
Later in the season, it gets complicated.
在这一季的后面，事情变得复杂了。

00:49:39.300 --> 00:49:43.300
I also remember Josh getting yelled at at TV Critics Association.
我也记得乔什在电视评论协会（TCA）上被怒斥的情形。

00:49:43.300 --> 00:49:56.265
Remember, we had a how I met your mother panel launching the pilot when the show got picked up, and all the critics went after the other, raised their hand, asked us questions for Q&A, and they were all like, so will you admit you made a mistake not making Rob in the mother?
还记得吗？当时剧集刚获预订，我们举办了一个《老爸老妈的浪漫史》见面会来宣传试播集，所有的评论人都冲了上来，举手在问答环节向我们提问，他们全都在说：“所以，你们肯承认把罗宾写成不是老妈是一个错误了吗？”

00:49:56.265 --> 00:49:58.198
They had such chemistry in the the pilot.
他们在试播集里有着那么强烈的化学反应。

00:49:58.198 --> 00:49:59.188
Do you remember this?
你还记得吗？

00:49:59.188 --> 00:50:02.677
I've done a helium style flash forward here where we're going to show you.
我这里做了一个类似快进的闪回，接下来要给你们看。

00:50:02.677 --> 00:50:03.177
Yeah.
对。

00:50:03.177 --> 00:50:07.986
And we're at a TV critics association event where you sort of introduce, we do a panel, there's a Q&A.
当时我们在一个电视评论协会的活动上，你们上台介绍，我们搞了一个座谈会，还有一个问答环节。

00:50:07.986 --> 00:50:08.646
And we got it.
然后我们挨了。

00:50:08.646 --> 00:50:09.146
Right.
对。

00:50:09.146 --> 00:50:10.042
They're mad at you.
他们生你的气。

00:50:10.042 --> 00:50:11.221
You're mad at me, Carter.
卡特，你生我的气。

00:50:11.539 --> 00:50:18.306
They were yelling at us and drilling us, setting the tone for many different moments where that would happen.
他们当时对我们大喊大叫、严厉质问，这也为后来许多类似时刻定下了基调。

00:50:18.306 --> 00:50:22.900
And I remember one guy saying, will you admit now that you made a mistake?
我记得有个家伙说：“你们现在肯承认自己犯了个错误吗？”

00:50:22.900 --> 00:50:28.689
Now that we see the chemistry between Ted and Robin, and now you're locked into this pilot ending that she's not the mother.
“既然我们都看到了泰德和罗宾之间的化学反应，而你们却又被试播集结尾锁死了，她不是老妈。”

00:50:28.689 --> 00:50:30.884
You had the future dad say it's not the mother.
你们让未来的老爸说她不是老妈。

00:50:30.884 --> 00:50:34.246
Are you serious considering before it airs going back and changing that?
在开播前，你们真的有认真考虑过回去把那段改掉吗？

00:50:34.246 --> 00:50:35.460
And she's just the mother.
让直截了当她就是老妈。

00:50:35.460 --> 00:50:36.490
And we're like, no.
而我们的反应是：不。

00:50:36.490 --> 00:50:39.582
And it was like, the show's over, the show's over, right?
当时的感觉就像是，这剧完了，这剧彻底完了，对吧？

00:50:39.582 --> 00:50:43.162
And it's like, well, then I was like, what is the show after that?
然后我就在想，那之后的故事该讲什么？

00:50:43.162 --> 00:50:47.827
The guy, they're like, well, could just be about them, they're, they're relationships.
那家伙的意思是，哦，那就只讲他们呗，他们、他们的关系。

00:50:47.827 --> 00:51:00.900
This is, this is, this is a, this is a bigger issue, which is is people don't like to live with ambiguity, they don't like to not know how something, they don't like the discomfort of, this didn't work out the way we thought it was going to.
这是一个、这是一个、这是一个更大的问题，那就是人们不喜欢带着模棱两可的感觉生活，他们不喜欢不知道某事会如何发展，他们不喜欢这种不适感——事情并没有按我们想象的那样发展。

00:51:00.900 --> 00:51:03.139
But you know what, neither does Ted.
但你知道吗，泰德也没有。

00:51:03.139 --> 00:51:05.099
Neither does Ted, neither does Ted.
泰德没有，泰德也没有。

00:51:05.099 --> 00:51:05.940
And neither does Ted.
泰德也没有。

00:51:05.940 --> 00:51:16.659
And again, like you being mad at the I love you moment, now watching it, when they went so many, when half the room was furious at us at this, it was like 300 TV critics, they were mad at us.
再说一次，就像你对“我爱你”那一刻感到生气一样，现在回看它，当时全场有一半人对我们气急败坏，现场大概有300名电视评论人，他们都在生我们的气。

00:51:16.659 --> 00:51:18.260
And we were getting yelled at.
我们当时被劈头盖脸地质问。

00:51:18.260 --> 00:51:23.099
I remember being like, Jesus Christ, I wish I had like had a couple whiskeys before coming out of the city.
我记得当时心想：天哪，我真希望在从城里过来之前先喝上几杯威士忌。

00:51:23.099 --> 00:51:26.019
And we were gonna be tired and feathered like what's going on.
我们感觉自己会被涂上柏油粘上羽毛（饱受羞辱），不知道会发生什么。

00:51:26.019 --> 00:51:32.179
And I remember having been told by the CBS publicist Beth, they go she was like, no, none of them are gonna want to ask you a question.
我记得CBS的公关贝丝曾对我们说过，她当时说：“不，他们没人会想问你们问题。”

00:51:32.179 --> 00:51:34.900
And they're gonna ask Neil about Doogie House or the rest of the house and about.
“他们只会问尼尔关于《天才小医生》或者其他剧的事情，还有关于……”

00:51:34.900 --> 00:51:38.420
Buffy, you'll get and you won't get two words and it was like first question.
“《吸血鬼猎人巴菲》的事，你们甚至连两句话都插不上。”结果第一句话就是：

00:51:38.420 --> 00:51:40.659
Hey, I want to talk to those motherfuckers that didn't get time.
“嘿，我想跟那帮没捞到时间的混蛋谈谈。”

00:51:40.659 --> 00:51:42.940
I'm gonna get it at the end of the month.
“我月底非得找他们算账不可。”

00:51:42.940 --> 00:51:45.860
Can they step forward and be shamed?
“他们能不能站出来接受羞辱？”

00:51:45.860 --> 00:51:50.698
I always think it's funny when you tell a storyteller creator, like, I consumed everything you did.
我一直觉得很好笑，当有人对一个讲故事的创作者说：我消费了你做的一切。

00:51:50.698 --> 00:51:56.368
I have, I read everything or I watch the whole thing and I am declaring to you that you did something wrong with it.
我读了你所有的东西或者看了全部内容，现在我向你宣布，你在里面做错了某件事。

00:51:56.368 --> 00:51:57.883
Here's where I would have done.
这是我会采取的处理方式。

00:51:57.883 --> 00:51:59.593
Here's how I would have written it.
这是我会写它的方式。

00:51:59.593 --> 00:52:01.108
It's like, it's not subjective.
这根本不是主观的。

00:52:01.108 --> 00:52:02.477
Write your own story, right?
你自己写个故事去啊，对吧？

00:52:02.477 --> 00:52:03.796
Do go write your own story.
去写你自己的故事啊。

00:52:03.796 --> 00:52:10.346
Yeah, and I, but I remember later that night when I finally had had a couple of whiskeys feeling like this was good that they got mad.
对，而且我记得那天深夜，当我终于喝了几杯威士忌之后，觉得他们生气其实是一件好事。

00:52:10.346 --> 00:52:13.474
Just like you said, it was good that you felt you were invested.
就像你说的，你觉得他们有代入感是件好事。

00:52:13.474 --> 00:52:16.260
So when Ted said I love you, even And now you got it mad.
所以当泰德说“我爱你”的时候，即使到现在，你还是会为之生气。

00:52:16.260 --> 00:52:18.260
It's a better than a difference.
总比无动于衷要好。

00:52:18.260 --> 00:52:21.340
Way better than boredom or indifference or swimmingness.
比无聊、冷漠或者平淡无奇要好得多。

00:52:21.340 --> 00:52:29.659
And I will say Alison Hannigan got us out of that, because we were getting harangued about well, you promised the mother and the title and then you fucked it up at the end.
而且我要说，艾莉森·汉妮根把我们从那个困境里拉出来了，因为我们当时正遭受严厉的质问：“你们在片名里承诺了老妈是谁，结果最后却把事情搞砸了。”

00:52:29.659 --> 00:52:33.179
And Alison Hannigan out this night where she goes, well guys, think about it.
就在那天晚上，艾莉森·汉妮根突然冒出一句：“嘿，各位，想想看。”

00:52:33.179 --> 00:52:36.539
On who's the boss, you never found out who the boss was.
“在《谁是老板》里，你们从来没搞清楚到底谁才是老板。”

00:52:36.539 --> 00:52:43.659
Everybody in the TCA, everybody in the TCA like cracked up laughing, like enormous laugh, enormous laugh.
TCA现场的所有人，TCA的每一个人都轰然大笑，笑得前仰后合，笑声震耳欲聋。

00:52:43.659 --> 00:52:45.380
And I was like, it saved us.
当时我想，这把我们救了。

00:52:45.380 --> 00:52:53.039
Also, Alison Hanigan, like every couple weeks would say the funniest thing I'd ever heard.
而且，艾莉森·汉妮根每隔几个星期就会说出我听过最好笑的话。

00:52:53.039 --> 00:52:56.000
But she would like, she was like stealth.
但她表现得很低调。

00:52:56.000 --> 00:52:58.239
She wasn't constantly cracking jokes.
她不是那种喋喋不休抖机灵的人。

00:52:58.239 --> 00:52:59.239
Yeah, she saved it.
对，她拯救了全场。

00:52:59.239 --> 00:53:00.239
Out of the blue.
突如其来地。

00:53:00.239 --> 00:53:01.239
She was a really good one.
她真的很有一套。

00:53:01.239 --> 00:53:02.239
She did save it.
她确实拯救了局面。

00:53:02.239 --> 00:53:04.079
And she would just, it would detonate.
而且她总是能一鸣惊人。

00:53:04.079 --> 00:53:06.760
It was so, she's going to be really funny.
太厉害了，她真的很搞笑。

00:53:06.760 --> 00:53:13.719
Her that joke lowered a helicopter ladder down and Carter and I just grabbed out of that joke and just were pulled out to save me.
她的那个笑话就像放下了一架直升机软梯，我和卡特一把抓住那个笑话，然后就被拉了上去，得救了。

00:53:13.719 --> 00:53:34.559
No, yeah, so we can introduce my lovely wife, Joanna, who is kind of part of the reason we're here.
不，对，所以我们可以介绍我可爱的妻子乔安娜，她可以说是我们来到这里的部分原因。

00:53:34.559 --> 00:53:37.000
She's a lover of TV, it's hilarious.
她是个狂热的电视迷，这太搞笑了。

00:53:37.000 --> 00:53:42.679
She's seen many TV shows, and for some reason, she had never seen a single episode of Howie Bay.
她看过无数部电视剧，但不知为何，她从来没看过哪怕一集《老爸老妈》。

00:53:42.679 --> 00:53:43.679
Another one.
又来一个。

00:53:43.679 --> 00:53:46.400
Not this one, which for me was a great relief when I met her.
不是这一部，这对我来说在我俩相遇时简直是个巨大的解脱。

00:53:46.400 --> 00:53:55.261
I mean, it's one of those things where if people have seen the show, you don't get a chance make a first impression with them.
我是说，这种感觉就像，如果人们看过这部剧，你就没有机会给他们留下第一印象了。

00:53:55.261 --> 00:53:58.425
They have this preconceived notion about you.
他们对你已经有了先入为主的观念。

00:53:58.425 --> 00:54:07.075
It was just so wonderful that she hadn't seen the show, so it really, I trusted that she was with me for the right reasons.
她没看过这部剧真是太好了，所以我真的确信她是出于正确的理由和我在一起的。

00:54:07.075 --> 00:54:12.280
But we are now embarking on this rewatch together, our first watch rather.
但我们现在开始一起重温这部剧了，或者说，这是她的首轮观看。

00:54:12.840 --> 00:54:14.842
We watched it the other night.
前几天晚上我们一起看了。

00:54:14.842 --> 00:54:19.982
I will say she, a couple of times when I was looking at Rob in a certain way.
我得说，有几次当我用某种眼神看着罗宾时。

00:54:19.982 --> 00:54:22.051
She would go, I know that look.
她会说：“我懂那个眼神。”

00:54:22.051 --> 00:54:26.591
I was going to ask, I was like, delicately going to try to ask that.
我正想问呢，我本来打算委婉地问一下这个。

00:54:26.591 --> 00:54:32.599
I'm thrilled you volunteered it because it's, you guys did a real chemistry in that pilot.
我很高兴你自己主动提出来了，因为你们俩在那个试播集里确实有着很强的化学反应。

00:54:32.599 --> 00:54:36.136
Again, 20 years before you ever met your, yeah, yeah.
再说一次，是在你遇见她之前的20年，对，对。

00:54:36.136 --> 00:54:38.473
No, she, I mean, she was delighted.
不，她，我的意思是，她很高兴。

00:54:38.473 --> 00:54:49.087
I think she can watch it with some objectivity and remove, you know, I don't think she's, I mean, it'd be silly for her to say, like, get away from that woman.
我觉得她可以带着一些客观性来看待它，抛开，你知道的，我不认为她会，我是说，如果她说“离那个女人远点”，那就太傻了。

00:54:49.087 --> 00:54:49.888
It was 2005.
那是2005年。

00:54:49.888 --> 00:54:50.555
Oh my God.
哦，天哪。

00:54:50.555 --> 00:54:53.426
Yeah, but she did record a question for us.
对，但她确实为我们录了一个问题。

00:54:53.426 --> 00:55:02.104
So she, she, I don't know if she'll ever be on here live, but she's going to slip off these voice notes so we can, we can hear it.
所以她，她，我不知道她以后会不会来现场，但她会发这些语音便签，这样我们就能，我们就能听到了。

00:55:02.104 --> 00:55:09.380
You use the word destiny at the end of the pilot, which is such a spiritual, powerful, meaning-centered word.
你在试播集结尾用了“命运”这个词，这是一个非常具有精神性、有力量、以意义为核心的词。

00:55:09.380 --> 00:55:24.600
And it's very comforting to think that we all have a destiny, that the twists and turns are happening to each of us for a reason, but I'm wondering about your personal beliefs, about destiny, your personal beliefs then, and now.
一想到我们所有人都有命运，所有的曲折对我们每个人来说都是事出有因，这让人感到非常安慰，但我想知道你对命运的个人看法，你当时的个人看法，以及现在。

00:55:24.599 --> 00:55:36.440
And also what it was like to write a show where you had total control over the destiny of five characters when we can feel so out of control when it comes to our own.
还有，编写一部你对五个角色的命运拥有完全掌控权的剧集是什么感觉，而我们在面对自己的命运时却会感到如此失控。

00:55:36.440 --> 00:55:40.599
It's such a smart question.
这是一个非常聪明的问题。

00:55:40.599 --> 00:55:46.400
We wrote this pilot, again, not really knowing if it would go beyond this.
我们写这个试播集的时候，同样，并不知道它是否会超越这个范围。

00:55:46.400 --> 00:55:50.639
And so we felt very much in the moment of that night.
所以我们当时非常沉浸在那天晚上的氛围中。

00:55:50.639 --> 00:55:54.570
We had ideas for where the show would go.
我们对这部剧的发展方向有过想法。

00:55:54.570 --> 00:56:03.449
But I never, even when we dove into really making it and having more and more ideas about how this would unfold, I never felt like masters of these characters destiny.
但我从来没有，甚至在我们真正投入制作并对剧情将如何展开有越来越多的想法时，我也从不觉得自己是这些角色命运的主宰。

00:56:03.449 --> 00:56:07.610
I think Carter and I wanted to let these characters surprise us.
我想我和卡特都希望让这些角色给我们带来惊喜。

00:56:07.610 --> 00:56:09.409
We wanted to respect these characters.
我们想要尊重这些角色。

00:56:09.409 --> 00:56:13.010
I came to have this delusional feeling like they were real people.
我甚至产生了一种近乎妄想的感觉，觉得他们是真人。

00:56:13.010 --> 00:56:16.409
I really like, I hear writers talk about that and I don't always feel that way.
我真的很喜欢，我听到编剧们谈论这种感觉，但我并不总是那样觉得。

00:56:16.409 --> 00:56:17.221
our right stuff.
我们擅长的那一套。

00:56:17.221 --> 00:56:21.530
This was like by the end of how much your mother, these were real human beings to me.
但到了《老爸老妈浪漫史》快结束时，对我和说，这些都是活生生的人。

00:56:21.530 --> 00:56:23.050
I didn't feel like some puppet master.
我感觉不到自己是什么木偶师。

00:56:23.050 --> 00:56:25.130
I felt like we were, I know, been Carter did either.
我觉得我们，我知道，卡特也没有这种感觉。

00:56:25.130 --> 00:56:29.130
I think we felt like these are our friends and we're kind of seeing what happens to them.
我想我们觉得这些是我们的朋友，我们只是在看他们身上发生了什么。

00:56:29.130 --> 00:56:30.434
We're kind of guiding them.
我们有点像在引导他们。

00:56:30.434 --> 00:56:33.769
Every now and again, we'd feel like, okay, we have to make something.
每隔一段时间，我们会觉得，好吧，我们得做点什么。

00:56:33.769 --> 00:56:36.811
We have to make something bad happen to one of them.
我们得让他们其中一个经历点糟糕的事。

00:56:36.811 --> 00:56:38.858
We need, we need an emotional turn.
我们需要，我们需要一个情感转折。

00:56:38.858 --> 00:56:41.841
We need to explore something hard and it felt evil.
我们需要探讨一些困难的事情，这感觉很残忍。

00:56:41.841 --> 00:56:42.602
It felt evil.
这感觉很残忍。

00:56:42.602 --> 00:56:51.610
That's part of what I get from and this question, we were controlling this destiny and we'd have to put them to this torture sometimes and especially Ted.
这正是我想说的，顺着这个问题，我们在控制这个命运，有时我们不得不让他们经历这种折磨，尤其是泰德。

00:56:51.610 --> 00:57:09.610
And we'd have to make our dear friend, Josh Radner, go into this kind of pain and do things like saying, blurting out I love you to Kobe Smolders in her apartment and the pilot and make them crawl through hard shit so we could come out the other side and all kind of learn something together.
我们不得不让我们亲爱的朋友乔什·拉德纳经历这种痛苦，做出一些举动，比如在试播集中在公寓里冲口对科比·史墨德斯说“我爱你”，让他们经历艰难的困境，这样我们才能熬过难关，一起学到一些东西。

00:57:09.610 --> 00:57:23.530
And often it was us making Carter myself, the other writers, stuff we took from Josh's life, other conversations we had with the actors, like we would take our own really hard shit and put the characters through some version of that.
而且通常情况是，卡特和我、还有其他编剧，会把从乔什生活中取材的东西、或者我们与演员的其他对话拿来用，我们会把自己真正经历的难关融入其中，让角色经历某种类似的遭遇。

00:57:23.530 --> 00:57:43.210
And so it felt shared, we tried to feel the pain with them, but was it strange to sort of come to feel like these were my friends almost like my family or my kids or something and then sometimes put them through hell but have this feeling like we knew it was all gonna work out somehow in the end, But even the end was complicated, right?
所以这感觉是共同经历的，我们努力去感同身受，但这种感觉是不是很奇怪——觉得这些是我的朋友，几乎就像我的家人或孩子一样，然后有时又把他们折磨得像在地狱一样，但又有一种感觉，我们知道到头来这一切都会解决的，不过结局也很复杂，对吧？

00:57:43.210 --> 00:57:53.889
And is there a neat bow on any chapter of life ever really quite, it just sort of keeps going and complicating and that sort of Ted's lesson in the pilot and in the series?
人生中的任何一个章节真的能画上一个完美的句号吗？它其实一直在继续，变得越来越复杂，这难道不正是泰德在试播集和整部剧中所学到的教训吗？

00:57:53.889 --> 00:58:01.090
And so I guess I felt a great deal of respect and a great, deep, almost delusional connection to these characters as we wrote them.
所以我想，在我们编写这些角色时，我怀着极大的敬意，以及一种非常深厚、几乎是妄想般的联系。

00:58:01.090 --> 00:58:11.170
And I felt like I was going through it with them and sort of cathartically putting some of my own shit or Carter's shit or whatever into these characters' lives and seeing what happened to them.
我觉得我正和他们一起经历这一切，并通过这种宣泄的方式，将我自己的一些破事、或者卡特的破事等等融入这些角色的生活中，看看他们会有什么遭遇。

00:58:11.170 --> 00:58:13.090
but it was always with great care.
但这一切始终都是极其小心翼翼的。

00:58:13.090 --> 00:58:14.250
Some of them as I felt very guilty.
有时我甚至会感到非常内疚。

00:58:14.250 --> 00:58:22.170
I felt guilty making Ted go like, what do you use that conversation you cited, Josh, where you're like, can't Ted just have a good moment and not be kicked in the nuts of the next episode?
我让泰德遭遇那些事时感到内疚，乔什，就像你提到的那场对话，你说：泰德难道就不能有个好时刻，而不是在下一集又被狠狠踢一脚吗？

00:58:23.210 --> 00:58:24.889
It's an interesting thing.
这是一件很有意思的事。

00:58:24.889 --> 00:58:30.489
It becomes a seduatiatorial Roman Gladiator like let's throw some lions in there and see what happens.
它变得有点像古罗马斗兽场，就像“让我们放几头狮子进去看看会发生什么”。

00:58:30.489 --> 00:58:38.849
Did you and Carter talk so more globally about the idea of Destiny as a major theme of the show?
你和卡特有没有更广泛地讨论过将“命运”作为该剧核心主题的想法？

00:58:38.849 --> 00:58:48.690
Yeah, I mean, we definitely, but also I think destiny as defined in how much mother to me is sort of the sense you make of things later.
对，我的意思是，我们绝对讨论过，但我也认为，对我而言，《老爸老妈浪漫史》中定义的命运，更像是你后来对事情的理解。

00:58:48.690 --> 00:58:57.050
I think in 2030, future Ted can see a sense of destiny and can see, let's, or magic, whatever you wanna call it.
我想在2030年，未来的泰德可以看到一种命运感，可以看到，或者说奇迹，随便你怎么称呼它。

00:58:57.050 --> 00:58:57.889
Or order.
或者秩序。

00:58:57.889 --> 00:59:01.849
Destiny sounds almost too neat for a lot of what happened on that show.
对于那部剧中发生的很多事情来说，“命运”这个词听起来未免太整齐划一了。

00:59:01.849 --> 00:59:10.981
I mean, destiny puts you in certain right places, at certain right times, and then life gets complicated some whole other way after that wonderful moment.
我的意思是，命运把你带到某些对的地方，在某些对的时间，然后在那段美好的时光之后，生活又以完全不同的方式变得复杂起来。

00:59:10.981 --> 00:59:24.620
I always love that that Kierkegaard quote that I think is really, I sound like Ted right now, but it's a really, it's so appropriate for how I made Mother that life can only be understood backward, but you have to live it forward.
我一直很喜欢克尔凯郭尔的那句名言，我觉得——我现在听起来很像泰德，但这真的很适合《老爸老妈浪漫史》，那就是生活只能倒过来理解，但必须向前生活。

00:59:24.620 --> 00:59:26.340
Yeah, yeah, that's the truth.
对，对，确实如此。

00:59:26.340 --> 00:59:30.075
So you get both of them in there, which we quoted in the pitch.
所以你同时拥有这两个状态，我们在提案里也提到了。

00:59:30.075 --> 00:59:34.404
You get, you get older Ted who knows, who knows where all this is headed.
你有年长一些的泰德，他知道、他知道这一切最终会走向何方。

00:59:34.404 --> 00:59:38.733
He knows all the twists and turns and he knows how he landed on his feet.
他经历了所有的曲折，也知道自己是如何化险为夷的。

00:59:38.733 --> 00:59:42.350
And then the Ted that I was playing doesn't know any of that.
而我当时扮演的那个泰德对这一切一无所知。

00:59:42.350 --> 00:59:47.924
He said that I was playing was like, this is the worst night of my life or this is calamitous.
我当时演的那个角色就像是：这是我生命中最糟糕的一晚，或者说这是场灾难。

00:59:47.924 --> 00:59:49.288
This is never I'm done.
这下完了，我彻底完了。

00:59:49.288 --> 00:59:51.126
I'm not going to be successful.
我不会成功了。

00:59:51.126 --> 00:59:54.447
I'm not going to have a family like all of these things.
我不会有家庭了，诸如此类的事情。

00:59:54.447 --> 01:00:00.969
I sometimes I used to think about like if you this is a weird thought experiment, but it pertains to the show.
我有时会想，如果你——这是一个奇怪的思想实验，但它和这部剧有关。

01:00:00.969 --> 01:00:13.849
So, if you were a molecule in a cell and you were like sentient, you knew you were a molecule in a cell and you could be interviewed and someone said, what's it like being a molecule in this cell?
那么，如果你是一个细胞里的分子，而且你是有意识的，你知道自己是细胞里的分子，并且你可以接受采访，有人问你：“在这个细胞里当一个分子是什么感觉？”

01:00:13.849 --> 01:00:21.449
You'd be like, it's insane, man, I'm getting knocked around, I don't know what's going on, it's just anarchy in here.
你会说：“太疯狂了，老兄，我到处挨撞，我不知道发生了什么，这里简直是一团糟。”

01:00:21.449 --> 01:00:28.849
But the scientist looking through a microscope sees that there's a real order to what's going on in that cell.
但是通过显微镜观察的科学家却能看出，那个细胞里发生的一切其实有着严密的秩序。

01:00:28.849 --> 01:01:09.889
He'll has a destiny of some sort like it's gonna right but the from the perspective of the molecule It doesn't know that yeah, and I always thought that was kind of like how I made him other like Ted was the molecule Getting knocked around inside the cell But then older Ted was kind of the scientist who's like no no I see how this is all coalescing and yes and gathering steam and and making sense, you know, yeah I think like that's destiny in the world of the show to me is like the gratitude you feel for for your life looking back and realizing that even the hard parts had to happen and they made you who you are and you survived them and you became who you are now and you're happy to be.
它某种程度上有着注定的命运，就像它会——对吧，但从分子的角度来看，它是不知道这一点的，是的。我一直觉得这有点像我是如何塑造他们的，比如泰德就像是在细胞内部到处碰撞的分子，但年长一些的泰德就像是那个科学家，他会说：“不，不，我能看出这一切是如何融合在一起的，是的，它们在积蓄力量、变得越来越有意义，你知道的，对吧。”我觉得在剧集的世界观里，命运这种东西，对我而言就像是你回首往事时对生活产生的感激，你会意识到即使是那些艰难的部分也是必须经历的，它们塑造了你，让你挺了过来，成就了现在的你，而且你为自己成为这样的人感到高兴。

01:01:09.889 --> 01:01:16.650
And you only achieved destiny when you're content where you are and you're accepting where you are and when you're young it's hard to feel that.
只有当你对现状感到满足和接纳时，你才真正达成了命运，而在年轻时，要感受到这一点是很难的。

01:01:16.650 --> 01:01:26.782
Yeah, and also I think because we live in such a kind of insanely upbeat and optimistic culture where we're shadow denying and death denying pain and suffering denying.
是的，而且我觉得也是因为我们生活在一个如此疯狂地追求积极乐观的文化中，我们都在否认阴暗面、否认死亡、否认痛苦与磨难。

01:01:26.782 --> 01:01:31.426
If you really ask people, what were the most meaningful moments of your life?
如果你真的去问人们：“你生命中最有意义的时刻是什么？”

01:01:31.426 --> 01:01:36.974
They often involve struggle and heartbreak and set back an unexpected turn of events, right?
它们往往伴随着挣扎、心碎、挫折以及意想不到的转折，对吧？

01:01:36.974 --> 01:01:42.101
They're never like, I graduated from business school and I'm like, girl, am I dreams?
人们从来不会说：“我从商学院毕业了，天哪，我的梦想实现了，而且一帆风顺。”

01:01:42.101 --> 01:01:43.367
And I had no problem.
而且我没有任何麻烦。

01:01:43.367 --> 01:01:48.614
Like, that's not what forges character, both in the larger sense of the word character.
那种经历并不能塑造性格，无论是广义上的性格。

01:01:48.614 --> 01:01:51.750
And in this specific sense, forges these characters.
还是在这个特定意义上，塑造了这些角色。

01:01:51.750 --> 01:02:03.209
So I think that I think they're that dual kind of consciousness of watching people live forward, not knowing how this is going to unfold and being quite scared but doing the best they could.
所以我认为，那种双重的意识——看着人们带着不确定命运将如何展开的未知感向前生活，虽然感到害怕却依然尽力而为。

01:02:03.210 --> 01:02:17.409
And then the older perspective of like almost like he's watching himself and saying, keep going kid, it's gonna, almost like me talking to myself when I watch the pilot, like, hey, yeah, you did okay, you did okay.
以及那种年长者的视角，就好像他在看着过去的自己并说：“坚持住，孩子，会过去的。”几乎就像我看着试播集时对自己说的那样：“嘿，是的，你做得不错，你做得挺好。”

01:02:17.409 --> 01:02:18.690
You landed on your feet.
你最终化险为夷了。

01:02:18.690 --> 01:02:29.280
So Craig, did we officially or did you officially rename the pilot the blue French horn?
所以克雷格，我们是不是正式地——或者说你是不是正式把试播集重新命名为《蓝色圆号》了？

01:02:29.280 --> 01:02:31.280
Yeah, in my mind I did.
对，我在心里是这么想的。

01:02:31.280 --> 01:02:32.440
How did I go?
我该怎么做？

01:02:32.440 --> 01:02:34.360
Is there a time machine so I can go do that?
有没有时光机能让我去把它改了？

01:02:34.360 --> 01:02:35.639
It should have been called that.
本来就该叫这个名字。

01:02:35.639 --> 01:02:37.559
I can admit it now.
我现在可以承认了。

01:02:37.559 --> 01:02:43.159
The one two punch of blue French horn and purple giraffe would have, I mean, you might have colors.
《蓝色圆号》和《紫色长颈鹿》的组合拳，我是说，你可能会喜欢各种颜色。

01:02:43.159 --> 01:02:44.159
It's all colors.
全都是颜色。

01:02:44.159 --> 01:02:46.480
You might have wanted every episode to have a color in it.
你可能希望每集都包含一种颜色。

01:02:46.480 --> 01:02:47.480
Damn it.
可恶。

01:02:47.480 --> 01:02:51.679
Every fucking pilot's called pilot and we should know there's a blue French horn.
每个该死的试播集都叫“试播集”，而我们本该知道应该叫《蓝色圆号》。

01:02:51.679 --> 01:02:53.599
Can we just, I just need that to be a retcon.
我们能不能——我只是需要把这变成官方设定（Retcon）。

01:02:53.599 --> 01:02:54.599
I don't know what to do about it.
我不知道该怎么处理这件事。

01:02:54.599 --> 01:02:55.880
All right, I'll talk to the studio.
好吧，我会去和制片厂谈谈。

01:02:55.880 --> 01:02:57.719
They may have a time machine.
他们说不定有时间机器。

01:02:57.719 --> 01:03:05.199
Before recording this episode, we asked you on Instagram from at how we made your mother.
在录制这集播客之前，我们在 Instagram 的 @howwemadeyourmother 账号上征集了大家的问题。

01:03:05.199 --> 01:03:11.949
If you had any questions about the pilot, by the time we had finished recording, an hour later, we had over 300 questions.
如果你对试播集有什么疑问，在我们的录制结束时，仅仅一个小时后，我们就收到了300多个问题。

01:03:11.949 --> 01:03:16.150
So thank you for sending those questions in.
所以非常感谢大家发来这些问题。

01:03:16.150 --> 01:03:28.269
So we can announce now that you can look forward to bonus episodes that we are calling general questions, general questions, where we rapid fire, go through as many of your questions as we can.
现在我们可以宣布，大家可以期待我们的特别节目了，我们称之为“常规问题”（General Questions），在节目中我们会以快问快答的形式，尽可能多地回答你们的问题。

01:03:28.269 --> 01:03:29.469
We'll be doing that each week.
我们每周都会这样做。

01:03:29.469 --> 01:03:34.869
So just look out on Instagram for a posting, asking you to submit those questions.
所以请关注 Instagram 上的发帖通知，按要求提交你们的问题。

01:03:34.869 --> 01:03:38.389
But also on the website, we're collecting your stories.
此外，我们也在官网上收集你们的故事。

01:03:38.389 --> 01:03:45.829
These are stories of how you discovered, how I met your mother, hard times I got you through, what generally what how I met your mother means to you.
这些故事讲述了你是如何发现《老爸老妈的浪漫史》的、它陪你度过了怎样的艰难时光，以及总的来说，《老爸老妈的浪漫史》对你意味着什么。

01:03:45.829 --> 01:03:54.429
So go to www.howwemadeyourmother.com, click on contact to submit your story.
请访问 www.howwemadeyourmother.com，点击“联系我们”来提交你的故事。

01:03:54.429 --> 01:03:56.070
And here is an amazing one.
这里有一个非常棒的故事。

01:03:56.070 --> 01:04:01.070
This is from Nephthes from Paris.
这是来自巴黎的奈芙蒂斯（Nephthes）发来的。

01:04:01.070 --> 01:04:04.190
Oh, this is like the greatest thing we've ever raised.
噢，这简直是我们做过最棒的节目。

01:04:04.190 --> 01:04:05.030
This is the one.
就是这个。

01:04:05.030 --> 01:04:06.670
We cheated and we read ahead on this one.
我们作弊了，提前看完了这篇。

01:04:06.670 --> 01:04:10.789
Yeah, this is quite an extraordinary letter.
对，这真是一封非同寻常的信。

01:04:10.789 --> 01:04:16.309
So we are so grateful for you, Nephthes, from, I hope I'm pronouncing it correctly.
所以我们非常感谢你，涅弗提斯（Nephthes），来自——希望我发音准确。

01:04:16.309 --> 01:04:17.550
I know, I don't know what to say.
天哪，我都不知道该说什么了。

01:04:17.550 --> 01:04:20.989
From Paris, sending this in salute.
来自巴黎，致以敬意。

01:04:20.989 --> 01:04:26.190
I was 12 years old the first time I stumbled upon how I met your mother.
我第一次无意中看到《老爸老妈浪漫史》时才12岁。

01:04:26.190 --> 01:04:27.909
It was 2013.
那是2013年。

01:04:27.909 --> 01:04:30.269
I was with my brother's girlfriends, little sister.
我和我哥哥女朋友的妹妹在一起。

01:04:30.269 --> 01:04:31.869
We were very close friends.
我们是非常要好的朋友。

01:04:31.869 --> 01:04:38.030
We had no money and spent our time inventing games, lives and careers for ourselves.
我们没钱，就把时间都花在为自己虚构游戏、生活和职业上。

01:04:38.030 --> 01:04:40.389
She had a tiny purple couch with green stripes.
她有一张带绿色条纹的微型紫色沙发。

01:04:40.389 --> 01:04:48.309
And sometimes we could watch series and movies on a tiny cathode rate TV that her mother had found at a flea market, if I remember correctly.
如果我没记错的话，有时我们可以在她妈妈在跳蚤市场找到的一个极小的显像管电视上观看剧集和电影。

01:04:48.309 --> 01:04:52.150
That's where we discovered our love for sitcoms, especially how I met your mother.
也就是在那里，我们发现了我们对情景喜剧的热爱，尤其是《老爸老妈浪漫史》。

01:04:52.150 --> 01:04:53.429
I love how the scene is set.
我很喜欢这种场景的设定。

01:04:53.429 --> 01:04:54.909
I love the description of the couch.
我很喜欢对那张沙发的描述。

01:04:54.909 --> 01:04:56.559
I'm already in.
我已经完全沉浸其中了。

01:04:56.559 --> 01:04:59.639
This series has been with me for so many years.
这部剧陪伴了我这么多年。

01:04:59.639 --> 01:05:05.090
I watch it when I'm happy, when I'm sad, when I'm sick, and when I'm perfectly fine.
我开心时看它，难受时看它，生病时看它，一切都好的时候也看它。

01:05:05.090 --> 01:05:08.409
I didn't grow up in a functional, happy, or loving family.
我没有在一个功能健全、幸福或充满爱的家庭中长大。

01:05:08.409 --> 01:05:14.943
How I met your mother taught me that this wasn't a life sentence, that home is wherever you choose to make it.
《老爸老妈浪漫史》教会我，这并不是无期徒刑，家是你选择建立它的地方。

01:05:14.943 --> 01:05:19.516
My home wasn't a place of shouting in tears, even if that's where I was born.
我的家不是一个充满争吵和泪水的地方，哪怕我就是在那里出生的。

01:05:19.516 --> 01:05:25.040
My home can be anywhere, with anyone, and I build it every day with that thought in my heart.
我的家可以随处都在，和任何人在一起，我每天都在心中怀着这个想法去构筑它。

01:05:25.040 --> 01:05:29.435
This show taught me not to give up and to cherish what brings joy in life.
这部剧教会我不要放弃，要珍惜生活中带来快乐的事物。

01:05:29.435 --> 01:05:43.690
Today I'm 23 years old, I live in Paris, and I'm a young journalist who meets her friend several times a week at the same bars, tells the same stories on repeat makes the same jokes and talks about love with her group in every conversation.
如今我23岁了，住在巴黎，是一名年轻的记者，每周和朋友去同一家酒吧好几次，反复讲着同样的故事，开着同样的玩笑，在每次谈话中都和闺蜜们聊着爱情。

01:05:44.329 --> 01:05:48.514
When I'm surrounded by this family I chose, sometimes often, I think of you.
当我被这个我选择的家庭环绕时，有时，甚至经常，我会想起你们。

01:05:48.514 --> 01:05:54.570
I tell myself that without this show, I might never have managed to escape the toxic environment I grew up in.
我告诉自己，如果没有这部剧，我可能永远无法摆脱我成长过程中的那个有毒的环境。

01:05:56.739 --> 01:06:00.082
I once shared my love for how I met your mother with a dear friend.
我曾和一个亲爱的朋友分享我对《老爸老妈浪漫史》的热爱。

01:06:00.082 --> 01:06:11.059
Our teenage days were filled with references, singing bang, bang, bang, bang at the top of our lungs, throwing slap bets left and right, granting each other favors without question and using weight for it in every sense.
我们的青春岁月充满了各种梗，扯着嗓子唱着“bang, bang, bang, bang”，左右开弓地打下巴掌赌，毫无疑问地满足对方的请求，并在各种意义上使用“等等我”。

01:06:12.019 --> 01:06:20.360
That friendship changed my life so intense, so complicated, that even though it ended long ago, I will always think of her whenever I think of you.
那段友谊改变了我的生活，它是如此强烈、如此复杂，以至于尽管它很久以前就结束了，每当我想到你们时，我总是会想起她。

01:06:20.360 --> 01:06:26.828
One night we slept over at each other's place and in the morning she left an unexpected gift, an unspoken message.
有一天晚上我们在彼此家里过夜，第二天早上她留下了一个意想不到的礼物，一个未言明的讯息。

01:06:26.828 --> 01:06:30.062
She placed a pineapple on my nightstand and I understood.
她把一个菠萝放在我的床头柜上，我懂了。

01:06:30.062 --> 01:06:34.601
My first love gave me a blue French horn necklace for our first Valentine's Day.
在我们的第一个情人节，我的初恋送了我一条蓝色法国号项链。

01:06:34.601 --> 01:06:38.062
I made him watch every season any cried for hours at the end.
我拉着他看完了每一季，结局时哭了好几个小时。

01:06:38.062 --> 01:06:46.516
I have forced dozens of people to watch this series and I've never left anyone unaffected because that's what how I met your mother represents to me.
我曾强迫几十个人看这部剧，而且从未让任何人无动于衷，因为这就是《老爸老妈浪漫史》对我的意义。

01:06:46.516 --> 01:06:54.969
Every person who has known me has heard me talk about this show and every person who no longer knows me will think of me whenever they hear its name.
每一个认识我的人都听我谈论过这部剧，每一个不再认识我的人只要听到它的名字就会想起我。

01:06:54.969 --> 01:06:59.530
I grew up with you at McLaren's and I will never stop being grateful for what you have given me.
我和你们一起在麦克拉伦酒吧长大，我永远不会停止感激你们所给予我的一切。

01:07:00.360 --> 01:07:03.559
Thank you Marshall for teaching me that kindness is the key to all relationships.
谢谢你，马歇尔，教导我善良是所有关系的关键。

01:07:03.559 --> 01:07:10.719
Thank you, Lily, for teaching me to be a loyal friend, always there for my loved ones, without losing myself in the process.
谢谢你，莉莉，教导我做一个忠实的朋友，永远陪伴在我爱的人身边，而在这个过程中不会迷失自我。

01:07:10.719 --> 01:07:15.679
Thank you, Robin, for teaching me that in the end, you have to be able to rely on yourself.
谢谢你，罗宾，教导我到头来，你必须能够依靠自己。

01:07:15.679 --> 01:07:19.400
Thank you, Barney, for reminding me how important it is to have fun.
谢谢你，巴尼，提醒我享受乐趣是多么重要。

01:07:19.400 --> 01:07:25.010
Thank you, Ted, for showing me that everything should be done with love never out of fear.
谢谢你，泰德，向我展示了所有事情都应该出于爱去做，而永远不要出于恐惧。

01:07:25.010 --> 01:07:26.809
Thank you all for everything.
谢谢大家所做的一切。

01:07:26.809 --> 01:07:30.809
You have been with me for two-thirds of my life, and this journey will likely never end.
你们陪伴了我生命的三分之二，这段旅程很可能永远不会结束。

01:07:30.809 --> 01:07:33.690
I could write for hours more, but it's already late.
我还可以再写几个小时，但时间已经很晚了。

01:07:33.690 --> 01:07:38.250
And as we all know, nothing good ever happens after 2am.
正如我们所知，凌晨两点之后绝不会发生什么好事。

01:07:38.250 --> 01:07:40.239
Thank you.
谢谢。

01:07:40.239 --> 01:07:41.239
Wow.
哇。

01:07:41.239 --> 01:07:47.110
I got choked up the first time I read that and I got choked up listening to it just now.
我第一次读到它时就哽咽了，刚才听的时候也哽咽了。

01:07:47.110 --> 01:07:48.110
Beautiful.
太美妙了。

01:07:48.110 --> 01:07:49.110
Beautiful.
太美妙了。

01:07:49.110 --> 01:07:50.659
That is why we made the show.
这就是我们制作这部剧的原因。

01:07:50.659 --> 01:07:56.579
Thank you for that beautiful message, really, really moved hearing that, really moved.
谢谢你带来这段美好的留言，真的，听到这个深受触动，真的很触动。

01:07:56.579 --> 01:07:57.579
Yeah.
是啊。

01:07:57.579 --> 01:08:18.199
I've read it a few times and I, yeah, I'm stunned at this sincerity and the, you know, I think back of like movies and TV, like sometimes it's filled with like toxic silly nonsense.
我读过好几次，我，是的，我为这份真诚感到震惊，而且，你知道的，我想起电影和电视，有时候里面充满了那种有毒的、愚蠢的废话。

01:08:18.199 --> 01:08:19.520
And some of this show was too.
这部剧的一些部分也是如此。

01:08:19.520 --> 01:08:21.680
And we're, I was honing for that.
而我们，我当时正在为此磨炼自己。

01:08:21.680 --> 01:08:34.609
But a lot of, you know, I learned how, like, I mean, I learned how to, I mean, I learned how to, you know, talk to women or even talk to friends.
但很多，你知道的，我学会了，就像，我的意思是，我学会了，我的意思是，我学会了，你知道的，如何与女性交谈，甚至如何与朋友交谈。

01:08:34.609 --> 01:08:42.569
Like there was so much that I learned from television and learned from, you know, when you fall in love with a TV series or fall in love with a movie.
就像我从电视中学到了那么多，从，你知道的，当你爱上一部电视剧或爱上一部电影时学到了那么多。

01:08:42.569 --> 01:08:44.010
I mean, it was so trippy for me.
我的意思是，这对我来说太魔幻了。

01:08:44.010 --> 01:08:47.810
Like I was such a fan of that first karate kid movie.
我竟然是那部第一部《小子难缠》电影的超级粉丝。

01:08:47.810 --> 01:08:54.010
Like to have Billy's abka and Ralph nacho on the show and like there on my phone now.
能让比利·扎布ka和拉尔夫·马基奥上节目，现在他们还在我的手机通讯录里。

01:08:54.010 --> 01:08:55.449
Like I really like those guys.
我是真的很喜欢这群家伙。

01:08:55.449 --> 01:08:56.609
They're fantastic.
他们太棒了。

01:08:56.609 --> 01:08:58.090
They're great.
他们很棒。

01:08:58.090 --> 01:09:12.250
Yeah, I mean, just the trippiness of that, but that move, I mean, talk about, I mean, Elizabeth Shoe, like, was like my first crush, or one of my first crushes, like, in that movie, I just remember how much I learned from film and TV.
是的，我的意思是，那种魔幻感，但那部电影，我的意思是，说到，伊丽莎白·苏，就像是我的初恋女神，或者我的初恋女神之一，在这部电影里，我只记得我从电影和电视中学到了多少东西。

01:09:12.250 --> 01:09:14.569
And again, not all of it was great lessons.
话说回来，并非所有都是什么好教训。

01:09:14.569 --> 01:09:25.010
I had to unlearn some of those lessons, but it's so incredible to be a part of a show that takes up permanent resonance within people's hearts and psyches, and I'm just really stunned by it.
我不得不忘掉其中一些教训，但能成为一部在人们心灵和精神中占据永久共鸣的剧集的一部分，这真是太不可思议了，我真的为此感到震惊。

01:09:25.010 --> 01:09:26.210
It is stunning.
这确实令人震惊。

01:09:26.210 --> 01:09:27.050
It's stunning.
太令人震惊了。

01:09:27.050 --> 01:09:28.341
I feel stunned by it.
我对此感到震惊。

01:09:28.341 --> 01:09:36.090
And so I had to have a fan like that who really got what the show was and then brought it around to all of these other people.
所以我必须拥有这样一个粉丝，她真正懂这部剧讲的是什么，然后把它传递给所有其他人。

01:09:36.090 --> 01:09:37.689
I mean, that is the dream.
我的意思是，这就是梦想。

01:09:38.329 --> 01:09:39.264
To cool to show.
酷毙了的剧。

01:09:39.264 --> 01:09:40.257
That's the dream.
这就是梦想。

01:09:40.257 --> 01:09:41.893
That's why we made the show.
这就是我们制作这部剧的原因。

01:09:41.893 --> 01:09:48.611
It was kind of a message in the bottle to find somebody exactly like this wonderful person who shared this message.
这就像是一个漂流瓶里的信息，为了找到一个像这个分享了这条信息的 maravilhoso（美妙的）人一样完美的粉丝。

01:09:48.611 --> 01:10:29.210
And part where they said it wasn't a life sentence to grow up in a hard place and a hard time and a hard home to grow up and then realize that it's not a life sentence that you can find a family and then sort of within this little letter of flashing forward to Paris in 23 years old and a young journalist who meets her friends several times a week at the bar and is kind of living her version of how much your mother it's just so epic and scope what the show kind of carried through that timeline of her life and just to think of it being an important part of that journey and getting and giving faith and hope and something to look forward to and realizing you're not stuck in a bad situation.
还有他们提到的那部分：在一个艰难的地方、艰难的时期和一个艰难的家庭中长大并不是无期徒刑，长大后意识到这不是无期徒刑，你可以找到一个家庭，然后在这个小纸条里，快进到23岁的巴黎，一个每周去酒吧几次并过着属于她自己的《老爸老妈浪漫史》版本的年轻记者，这在她人生的那个时间线中所承载的宏大叙事真是太史诗级了，只要想到它成为那段旅程的重要部分，并获得并给予信念、希望以及对未来的期盼，并意识到你并没有陷入糟糕的困境中。

01:10:29.210 --> 01:10:31.962
You can choose your way into better situations.
你可以选择通往更好境遇的道路。

01:10:31.962 --> 01:10:36.120
And, you know, the idea that the show inspired, that is really amazing.
而且，你知道的，这部剧所激发的这种想法，真的很了不起。

01:10:36.120 --> 01:10:38.170
Thank you for sharing that message.
谢谢你分享这条留言。

01:10:38.970 --> 01:10:40.324
It also strikes me, Craig.
这也让我深有感触，克雷格。

01:10:40.324 --> 01:10:53.449
It's like, you know how there's that kind of, I mean, speaking of Star Wars, which you know a lot better than I do, but like that kind of charge where people say like fans understand the series better than George Lucas does at some like cellular level.
这就好像，你知道那种，我的意思是说到《星球大战》，你比我了解得多得多，但就像那种人们常说的粉丝在某种细胞层面上比乔治·卢卡斯更理解这个系列的指控。

01:10:53.449 --> 01:10:56.935
like not that you and Carter don't understand the show.
并不是说你和卡特不了解这部剧。

01:10:56.935 --> 01:11:06.759
I just feel like at some point when a show permeates into the culture, it's actually belongs to the fans more than it belongs to the people who created it.
我只是觉得，当一部剧渗透到文化中时，它实际上比属于创造它的人更属于粉丝。

01:11:06.759 --> 01:11:19.688
And the fans actually are going to have insight into things and make connections and draw like certain parallels that you didn't even see or themes that they can tease out that they've watched it so much.
而且粉丝实际上会对某些事情有独到的见解，能建立联系，并画出一些你甚至都没看到的平行线，或者因为看了太多遍而能梳理出一些主题。

01:11:19.688 --> 01:11:56.319
I mean genuinely when people come up to me and say I've seen the show 17 times like I have not seen all these episodes like they know this show way better than I know this show Or they'll throw like an old joke at me and I'm like, I don't know what that is And they're like yeah, they think I'm lying like they think that I it's like part of my like DNA That you know, yeah, I don't like I don't remember that it was it was it was 2008 But it does feel like the fans own it And in some ways, we're going back and saying to the fans, hey, tell us what this thing is that we made.
我的意思是，当人们走过来对我说“我看过这部剧17遍了”时，我是真心觉得我都没有看过所有这些剧集——他们比我更懂这部剧，或者他们会向我抛出一个老梗，而我会说：“我不知道那是什么。”然后他们就会觉得——是的，他们觉得我在撒谎，他们觉得我——这就好像是我DNA的一部分，你知道的，是的，我不记得了，那是2008年的事了。但感觉确实是粉丝拥有了它。在某些方面，我们正在回过头来对粉丝说：“嘿，告诉我们我们制作的这个东西到底是什么。”

01:11:56.319 --> 01:11:57.319
Tell us what this is.
告诉我们这到底是什么。

01:11:57.319 --> 01:11:59.960
That is what, that's what this whole podcast is.
这就是，这就是整个播客的意义所在。

01:11:59.960 --> 01:12:05.079
I'm loving it so much because at the time I identified, I over identified with it.
我太喜欢它了，因为当时我认同了它，我过度认同了它。

01:12:05.079 --> 01:12:06.279
I thought, this is me.
我当时想，这就是我。

01:12:06.279 --> 01:12:12.640
This is the, you know, I am only identified by this thing we're making in this moment in my 30s.
这就是，你知道的，我30多岁时正在创作的这个东西才是我唯一的身份认同。

01:12:12.640 --> 01:12:13.640
And this is my shot.
这就是我的机会。

01:12:13.640 --> 01:12:16.720
And this is how every up and down, I'll take it all like it's me.
这就是每一个起起落落，我都全盘接受，就好像它是我一样。

01:12:16.720 --> 01:12:21.960
I feel like I've failed if it's not great, et cetera, or, you know, the lows are always lower than the highs.
我觉得如果它不够伟大，我就是失败了，诸如此类，或者说，你知道的，低谷总是比高峰更低。

01:12:21.960 --> 01:12:24.479
But you'll be identified entirely by it.
但你会被它完全定义。

01:12:24.479 --> 01:12:31.500
There's such freedom and just what a gift to realize, it's so much bigger than any one person.
能意识到它比任何一个人都要宏大，这是一种巨大的自由，也是一份多么棒的礼物。

01:12:31.500 --> 01:12:34.800
It's not me with the show finished 10, 11 years ago now.
这部剧完结已经10、11年了。

01:12:34.800 --> 01:12:38.520
And it's finding people and it's becoming theirs.
它正在找到属于它的人，并成为他们的一部分。

01:12:38.520 --> 01:12:46.399
And to go back into it from outside now and experiencing it through the fans eyes and realizing what it meant to people.
如今回过头来从外部重新审视，通过粉丝的视角去体验，并意识到这对于人们意味着什么。

01:12:46.399 --> 01:13:06.640
And into humbling in the best possible way, it makes me feel so small and like we were just, it was our job just to channel this bigger thing, capture it in this moment of time where we're so lucky to all be in the same place at the same time, picking up on the same radio of signal together from the universe and sort of channel it into this thing, send it out into the world and see what happens.
这种感觉以最好的方式让人感到谦卑，它让我觉得自己如此渺小，就像我们的工作只是去引导这个更宏大的事物，将它捕捉在我们都如此幸运地在同一时间身处同一地点的这一时刻，共同接收来自宇宙的同一个无线电信号，然后将其引导成这个事物，发送到世界各地，看看会发生什么。

01:13:06.640 --> 01:13:08.479
And this is the part where we get to see what happened.
而这就是我们得以看到发生了什么的部分。

01:13:08.479 --> 01:13:09.479
And it's wonderful.
而且这太棒了。

01:13:09.479 --> 01:13:11.293
I am guilty.
我有罪。

